The primary purpose of an **EMM INFORMATION** message is for the network to provide the UE with **network-related display and time information**, without changing the UE’s EMM registration state.

It can carry, for example:

- Full and/or short **network name** for display to the user;
- **Local time zone**;
- **Universal time and local time zone**;
- **Daylight saving time** indication.

It is therefore mainly used to allow the UE to present the correct operator identity and local date/time information to the user. It is an informational, network-initiated NAS message—not a registration, authentication, or bearer-control procedure.

When the MME receives a **SECURITY MODE COMPLETE** containing a **Replayed NAS message container** with a **TRACKING AREA UPDATE REQUEST**, it shall treat the encapsulated TAU REQUEST as the UE’s pending TAU request and process it.

Procedurally:

- successful receipt of SECURITY MODE COMPLETE completes security-mode control and activates the new EPS NAS security context;
- the MME then processes the replayed **TRACKING AREA UPDATE REQUEST**;
- the MME proceeds with the **tracking area updating procedure**—e.g. subscriber/context checks and, if accepted, sends a **TRACKING AREA UPDATE ACCEPT** (or a TAU REJECT if applicable).

The TAU REQUEST is not treated as a new separately received unprotected NAS message requiring another security-mode-control exchange. It is replayed inside SECURITY MODE COMPLETE specifically so that the original TAU procedure can continue after NAS security has been established.

A UE can be in **EMM-REGISTERED.ATTEMPTING-TO-UPDATE-MM** after a combined procedure because a *combined TA/LA update* is still, from the EPS NAS/EMM viewpoint, a **tracking area updating (TAU) procedure**.

The “combined” aspect only means that the UE requested that the network also update its CS-domain location (LA) via the SGs interface, where applicable. It does not make the EPS mobility-management update complete merely because the request was sent.

The UE enters **EMM-REGISTERED.ATTEMPTING-TO-UPDATE-MM** when it has initiated a TAU, including a combined TA/LA update, for example because:

- it entered a tracking area that is not in its registered TA list;
- periodic TAU is due;
- the network requested a TAU;
- a previous registration/update condition requires a new TAU;
- it needs to perform a combined TA/LA update for CS-domain registration.

It remains in that state while the TAU is outstanding, such as while it is:

- waiting for a **TRACKING AREA UPDATE ACCEPT**;
- undergoing NAS authentication, security mode control, or identity procedures associated with the TAU;
- retransmitting the TAU Request under **T3430**;
- waiting to retry after an access/RRC/NAS delivery failure, subject to the applicable retry and back-off rules.

A successful combined TA/LA update normally results in a TAU Accept whose update result indicates that the combined update was performed. The UE can then move to **EMM-REGISTERED.NORMAL-SERVICE** (assuming normal service is available).

Therefore, seeing **EMM-REGISTERED.ATTEMPTING-TO-UPDATE-MM** after a “combined procedure” generally means that the combined TAU has been started but its EPS/MM portion has not yet been successfully completed, or is being retried. It does **not** by itself mean that the UE is deregistered, nor does it necessarily indicate failure of the CS/LA part; it indicates an outstanding EPS mobility update.

The **N1 UE network capability IE** is used by a UE operating in **EPS/S1 mode** to provide the EPC/MME with the UE capabilities that are relevant when the UE operates in **N1 mode**, i.e. with 5GS NAS/5GC.

Its purpose is therefore to support **EPS–5GS interworking and mobility**:

- the MME can store the UE’s N1/5GS-related network capability information while the UE is attached or registered in EPS;
- when the UE moves from EPS to 5GS, the capability information can be made available to the AMF as part of UE context handling;
- the 5GS can then apply the appropriate procedures and feature handling without having to infer those capabilities merely from the EPS UE network capability.

It is a **capability indication**, not an indication that the UE is currently connected over N1 or a request to establish an N1 NAS connection. It is essentially the EPS-side counterpart of providing **S1 UE network capability** information when the UE is in 5GS.

TS 24.301 specifies the *behaviour* associated with NAS back-off timers, rather than mandating a particular software or hardware timer implementation in the UE.

In practical terms, the UE implementation must ensure that, after receiving a NAS rejection carrying a back-off value, the UE remembers the restriction and suppresses the relevant retransmission/reattempts until expiry.

The main NAS back-off timers are:

- **T3346** — EMM congestion back-off timer;
- **T3396** — ESM/PDN connectivity back-off timer.

### T3346 — EMM back-off

T3346 is used when the network rejects an EMM procedure for congestion-related reasons and provides a **T3346 value** IE, for example in an `ATTACH REJECT`, `TAU REJECT`, or applicable `SERVICE REJECT`.

The UE is required to:

- start T3346 with the value supplied by the network;
- apply the resulting access restriction to the relevant PLMN;
- refrain from initiating the EMM procedures that are barred while the timer is running, notably new normal attach/TAU/service-request attempts as applicable to the rejection case;
- retain the normal ability to make attempts which are explicitly exempt, in particular emergency-service-related procedures where permitted;
- resume normal attempts when the timer expires, or when another specified condition stops/overrides the timer.

Thus, a UE must not treat the rejection as merely a failed transaction and immediately retry it. It must enforce the network-requested quiet period.

If the rejection case specifies a default back-off value when no T3346 value is included, the UE uses that specified default rather than choosing an arbitrary retry interval.

### T3396 — PDN connectivity / ESM back-off

T3396 is the equivalent ESM-level mechanism. It is started when a `PDN CONNECTIVITY REJECT` includes a back-off timer value in a case where the network is asking the UE not to retry PDN connectivity.

While T3396 is running, the UE must not initiate another PDN connectivity procedure for the affected APN (and, where applicable, the associated PDN type/request context). This does not mean that all NAS or all packet services are necessarily barred: the restriction is scoped to the rejected PDN connectivity request.

An implementation therefore needs to associate the ESM back-off condition with the relevant APN/context, rather than globally disabling all PDN requests without regard to which APN was rejected.

### Implementation principle

The important specification point is that a NAS back-off timer is a **network-enforced retry suppression mechanism**, not simply a local retransmission timer such as T3410 or T3480. The UE implementation must therefore:

1. decode and validate the received timer value;
2. start the appropriate back-off timer;
3. maintain the associated barred-procedure state for the timer duration;
4. prevent prohibited NAS procedure initiation while it runs;
5. allow only the exceptions explicitly specified by TS 24.301;
6. clear the restriction on expiry or on another explicitly specified stopping condition.

The standard does not require a particular internal architecture—e.g. a physical timer versus a software deadline—but the externally visible result must be that the UE does not reattempt the barred procedure before the applicable back-off period has ended.

A developer must consult TS 24.008 because TS 24.301 does not independently define the contents of the **Mobile station classmark 3** information element.

TS 24.301 uses the IE in EPS/LTE NAS contexts where GERAN/CS-domain capability information may be needed, but it normatively refers to the TS 24.008 definition of Mobile station classmark 3. TS 24.008 therefore supplies the actual technical rules needed to implement it, including:

- the IE structure and length encoding;
- the meaning of the octets/bits;
- optional and extension octets;
- rules for presence of capability fields;
- interpretation of GERAN radio capabilities, such as supported bands, channel/multislot capabilities, ciphering-related capabilities, and other GERAN-specific features;
- handling of unknown, absent, spare, or truncated portions of the IE.

In other words, TS 24.301 tells the implementer **when and in what EPS NAS context** the classmark 3 IE is used, while TS 24.008 tells the implementer **how to encode, decode, and interpret the classmark 3 payload**.

This matters particularly for LTE devices supporting CS fallback and/or GERAN interworking. Treating classmark 3 as an LTE-native capability IE, or inventing its format from TS 24.301 alone, would risk incompatible encoding and incorrect network capability decisions.

The cross-reference to TS 24.008 means that the **Mobile station classmark 3** information element used in LTE NAS is a legacy GERAN/CS-domain capability IE whose **format, coding, and capability-bit meanings are defined by TS 24.008**, rather than being redefined in TS 24.301.

Its purpose in TS 24.301 is to let an LTE/EPC network obtain the UE’s relevant GERAN radio capabilities when those capabilities may matter—for example, for inter-RAT operation or CS fallback toward GERAN. The UE carries the Classmark 3 IE in the relevant EMM message when applicable; the receiving network interprets its contents according to TS 24.008.

Thus, the reference is primarily:

- an **encoding/semantics reference** to the GSM/GERAN NAS specification;
- a way to preserve compatibility with the existing GERAN classmark definition;
- not an indication that LTE NAS itself performs TS 24.008 mobility procedures or that Classmark 3 is universally required in every LTE attach/update.

A UE may initiate a Tracking Area Update (TAU) after changing from **S101 mode** to **S1 mode** because it has returned to normal EPS/S1 access and the EPS mobility-management entity needs to re-establish/update the UE’s EPS mobility and location context.

In S101 mode, the UE is operating using the interworking arrangement associated with the S101 interface rather than normal S1 access. When it moves back to S1 mode, the UE may need to inform the MME that:

- it is again reachable through the S1/E-UTRAN system;
- its current tracking area and serving access may have changed while it was in S101 mode;
- the MME should update the UE’s registered location, reachability, and related EPS context.

Thus, TS 24.301 includes the inter-system change from **S101 mode to S1 mode** as a TAU trigger. The TAU is not necessarily caused by expiry of the periodic TAU timer or by entering an unlisted TA; it can be caused directly by the return from S101 to S1 mode so that EPS mobility management is synchronized again.

Before the UE activates NAS integrity protection using the security context selected by the network, it must first validate the received **SECURITY MODE COMMAND**.

In particular, the UE shall check that:

- the SECURITY MODE COMMAND is **integrity protected** and that its MAC/integrity check is successful, using the NAS security context indicated by the message/security-header processing rules;
- the indicated NAS key set identifier refers to an available and valid EPS NAS security context;
- the selected NAS integrity and ciphering algorithms are supported by the UE and are permitted for the procedure;
- the **UE security capability** replayed in the SECURITY MODE COMMAND is consistent with the UE security capability that the UE sent in the relevant initial NAS request (e.g. ATTACH REQUEST or TRACKING AREA UPDATE REQUEST). This is the anti-algorithm-downgrade check.

If the integrity protection check fails, the UE discards the command. If the selected algorithms are unacceptable or the replayed UE security capabilities do not match, the UE rejects the security-mode procedure as specified (for example with the appropriate SECURITY MODE REJECT cause).

Only after these checks succeed does the UE activate NAS integrity protection with the selected NAS integrity algorithm and send **SECURITY MODE COMPLETE** protected under that security context.

The purpose is to bootstrap the ProSe security/key-management relationship needed for UE-to-network relay operation.

A ProSe UE-to-network relay must be able to contact the appropriate ProSe Key Management Function (PKMF) in order to obtain or manage the ProSe security material/authorization used for relay-related PC5 operation with remote UEs. The PKMF is operator- and subscription-dependent; it is not necessarily a fixed, globally known address. Supplying its address allows the relay UE to reach the correct PKMF after registration/attachment or after a change of serving MME.

Importantly, in the normal TS 24.301 NAS usage, the relay UE does **not** originate the PKMF Address merely because it is a relay. The UE indicates its ProSe/relay capability or relay-service support in the relevant NAS request. The MME, after checking subscription/provisioning information, provides the **ProSe Key Management Function Address IE** to the UE in the applicable NAS response (for example, attach/TAU accept when the relevant conditions are met).

Thus, the IE is:

- a bootstrap/configuration item for the relay UE;
- used to identify the PKMF serving that UE’s ProSe relay security context;
- not itself proof of authorization, and not a user-plane routing address for remote-UE traffic;
- sent only when the network has the relevant PKMF address and the UE is eligible/configured for the corresponding ProSe relay functionality.

A receiver may ignore (discard) an entire NAS message when it cannot be validly or safely processed, for example because:

- the message type is not compatible with the current EMM/ESM state;
- a mandatory information element is missing, invalid, or has an incorrect length;
- an unknown information element is received that is marked as comprehension required;
- the message is otherwise malformed or contains a protocol error that prevents correct interpretation.

The receiver must not perform the procedure requested by the discarded message. It shall normally report the error by sending the appropriate NAS status message:

- **EMM STATUS** for EMM-related errors; or
- **ESM STATUS** for ESM-related errors,

including the applicable cause, e.g. *invalid mandatory information*, *message type non-existent or not implemented*, or *message type not compatible with protocol state*.

An important exception is a message failing NAS security verification, such as an integrity-check failure: it is discarded and typically no NAS STATUS is sent in response, since replying could itself be inappropriate or exploitable.

The **PDN type** IE identifies the type of PDN connectivity the UE is requesting, i.e. the IP protocol version/address-family type for the PDN connection.

It lets the UE indicate whether it wants, for example:

- an **IPv4** PDN connection;
- an **IPv6** PDN connection;
- an **IPv4v6** (dual-stack) PDN connection; and, where applicable in the release, other defined PDN types.

It is therefore used by the network when establishing the PDN connection to determine what kind of PDN address/connectivity is requested. It does **not** itself carry the assigned PDN address; that address is conveyed separately in the relevant PDN address information.

A **NAS message container IE** that is specified to carry a *complete plain NAS message* is an encapsulation mechanism for NAS message contents, not a second NAS security transport layer.

A **SECURITY PROTECTED NAS MESSAGE** is different from a plain NAS message: it includes a NAS security header, MAC and sequence number, and possibly ciphered NAS payload. Its protection is bound to the NAS security context and NAS COUNT used for an actual NAS PDU exchanged between the UE and MME.

Therefore, putting a security-protected NAS message inside such an IE would create unsupported nested NAS security processing:

- the outer NAS PDU already has the applicable NAS security treatment;
- the inner message would contain another security header, MAC and sequence number;
- the receiver would have to decide whether and how to perform a second integrity/ciphering operation and how to handle the inner NAS COUNT;
- this would break the normal one-PDU/one-security-header processing model and could create sequence-number, replay-protection, and security-context ambiguity.

Thus, when the IE says it contains a “complete plain NAS message,” the contained message must have the plain NAS security header form (security header type indicating no security protection). A SECURITY PROTECTED NAS MESSAGE must be sent as the NAS PDU to which NAS security processing applies, not embedded inside a plain-NAS-message container.

The **NBIFOM container** IE is included in TS 24.301 to provide a NAS transport mechanism for **Network-Based IP Flow Mobility (NBIFOM)** signaling.

NBIFOM allows a UE with simultaneous 3GPP and non-3GPP access connectivity, for example LTE and trusted/untrusted WLAN, to keep the same PDN connection/IP address while the network moves or steers individual IP flows between the accesses. Unlike UE-controlled IFOM, the mobility decision and flow routing are primarily network controlled.

The NAS layer itself does not interpret NBIFOM procedures or flow-binding contents. Therefore, TS 24.301 defines the NBIFOM container as an essentially transparent ESM/NAS payload that can carry NBIFOM signaling between the UE and the network entity controlling the PDN connection, typically the PGW/PCEF-related function, with the MME acting as the NAS relay.

It is needed to convey information such as NBIFOM-related requests, responses, and indications associated with:

- enabling or updating NBIFOM operation for a PDN connection;
- informing the UE of access/flow-mobility handling;
- coordinating movement or steering of IP flows between 3GPP and non-3GPP accesses;
- preserving the PDN connection and IP continuity while the selected access for a flow changes.

The IE is therefore not present for ordinary EPS bearer establishment or normal LTE mobility. It is included only when NBIFOM is supported and NBIFOM-related signaling needs to be transported. Its presence does **not** mean that NAS or the MME performs per-flow routing; it allows the NBIFOM-capable endpoints to exchange the required flow-mobility information through NAS.

The EMM (EPS Mobility Management) sublayer is the NAS control-plane function that coordinates UE registration, reachability, mobility, NAS security, and the availability of NAS signalling for ESM procedures. It does not carry user-plane traffic itself.

Its interaction with other layers/entities can be summarized as follows.

### 1. Interaction with the ESM sublayer

EMM and ESM are the two NAS sublayers:

- **EMM** handles mobility-management and NAS connection-management functions, e.g. attach, detach, tracking area update, service request, authentication, and NAS security.
- **ESM** handles session and bearer-management functions, e.g. PDN connectivity, EPS bearer establishment/modification/release, and QoS-related bearer control.

EMM provides the conditions and transport needed for ESM signalling:

- ESM procedures normally require the UE to be registered and, where necessary, to have an NAS signalling connection.
- If ESM needs to send an ESM message while no suitable NAS/RRC connection exists, EMM establishes the required signalling connectivity, typically through an RRC connection establishment and an EMM service request or attach-related procedure.
- EMM transports ESM messages over NAS. For example:
  - an initial **PDN CONNECTIVITY REQUEST** may be included in an **ATTACH REQUEST**;
  - later ESM messages can be carried in **UL NAS TRANSPORT** and **DL NAS TRANSPORT** messages;
  - some ESM messages can be included in other EMM procedures where the specification permits an ESM message container.
- EMM applies NAS security protection to NAS messages once NAS security has been activated. This protection covers the ESM information transported within NAS as well.
- EMM does not perform ESM bearer/session decisions itself. It routes the ESM payload to the ESM entity; ESM interprets and acts on the ESM protocol message.

Thus, ESM can request session/bearer procedures, but EMM controls whether the UE is sufficiently registered, reachable, and NAS-connected to perform the signalling.

---

### 2. Interaction with the RRC/Access Stratum

EMM uses the Access Stratum, principally RRC, as the transport path for NAS messages.

RRC provides functions such as:

- establishment and release of the RRC connection;
- transfer of NAS PDUs between UE and MME;
- paging and UE access procedures;
- radio bearer and access-stratum security handling.

EMM relies on RRC for NAS message delivery:

- In an idle/inactive access situation, the UE may first establish an RRC connection in order to send an EMM message such as an Attach Request, Tracking Area Update Request, or Service Request.
- Once an RRC connection is available, EMM supplies NAS PDUs to RRC for transmission.
- On the downlink, RRC delivers received NAS PDUs to EMM or ESM according to the NAS protocol discriminator/message type.
- RRC indications, such as loss of radio connection, cell/TA changes, paging, or access failure, can cause EMM to start, defer, retry, or abort EMM procedures.

EMM does not directly control radio resources or user-plane bearers. It requests or uses NAS signalling transport; RRC and lower layers perform the radio-side procedures.

---

### 3. Interaction with NAS security and authentication functions

EMM is responsible for NAS-level security control. It:

- initiates and processes authentication;
- selects and activates the NAS security context through the Security Mode Control procedure;
- maintains NAS security context state;
- applies NAS integrity protection and, where applicable, NAS ciphering;
- handles security context updates, failures, and re-establishment rules.

This is important for both EMM and ESM because, after NAS security activation, ESM signalling is normally transported under the NAS security context controlled by EMM.

EMM security is distinct from Access Stratum security:

- **EMM/NAS security** protects NAS signalling between UE and MME.
- **AS security** protects radio-interface signalling and user-plane traffic between UE and eNB.

The two are coordinated during connection establishment and security setup, but they are different security domains.

---

### 4. Interaction with upper-layer applications and UE functions

Upper layers do not normally exchange 24.301 protocol messages directly with EMM. Instead, EMM exposes the operational result of mobility management to UE functions and applications, for example:

- whether the UE is registered for EPS services;
- whether normal, emergency, or limited service is available;
- whether the UE is reachable for mobile-terminated services;
- whether a service request or registration is required before data services can proceed;
- network-provided information such as identity, tracking area, and certain mobility-related indications.

Applications typically trigger IP/data service needs through the UE’s packet-data stack. That need may lead ESM to request PDN/bearer procedures, with EMM ensuring that the necessary registration and NAS connectivity exist.

---

### 5. Interaction with the peer EMM entity in the network

The UE EMM entity communicates with the MME EMM entity by EMM NAS procedures. Examples include:

- Attach;
- Detach;
- Tracking Area Update;
- Service Request;
- Authentication;
- Security Mode Control;
- Identity procedures;
- EMM Information;
- GUTI reallocation.

The UE and MME each maintain EMM state and security context. Their state handling must remain consistent. For example:

- a successful attach moves the UE from deregistered to registered operation;
- a tracking-area change may require a TAU;
- an incoming paging/service need may require a service request;
- authentication or security failure may prevent normal ESM activity until resolved;
- detach, reject, or network barring may remove or restrict service availability.

The internal interfaces between the MME EMM function and entities such as HSS, S-GW/P-GW, eNB, or S1AP are not NAS air-interface procedures. They support EMM decisions, but TS 24.301 primarily specifies the UE–MME NAS behaviour.

---

### 6. State-dependent behavior

The interaction is strongly dependent on EMM state and connectivity.

- **EMM-DEREGISTERED:** Normal ESM service is generally unavailable until attach/registration succeeds, except for cases such as emergency procedures where specifically allowed.
- **EMM-REGISTERED with no active signalling connection:** ESM or network activity may require RRC establishment and an EMM service request before signalling can proceed.
- **EMM-REGISTERED with signalling connectivity:** ESM messages can normally be transported directly over NAS.
- **During attach, TAU, detach, authentication, or security procedures:** EMM may restrict, sequence, delay, or reject other requests according to the applicable procedure rules and timers.

In short, EMM is the NAS control coordinator: it uses RRC/AS for delivery, provides secure and reachable NAS transport for ESM, maintains mobility and registration state with the MME, and informs higher UE functions of EPS service availability.

The **T3324 value IE (“active time”) is a request/negotiation parameter, not an entitlement**. The UE may include a requested T3324 value in an `ATTACH REQUEST` or `TRACKING AREA UPDATE REQUEST`, but the MME decides whether PSM/active-time operation is allowed and, if so, what value to assign.

Therefore, the MME can omit the T3324 value IE in the corresponding `ATTACH ACCEPT` or `TAU ACCEPT` when it does not allocate an active time, for example because:

- PSM is not supported or not enabled in that MME/PLMN;
- the UE/subscription is not authorized for PSM;
- PSM is restricted due to roaming, operator policy, congestion, service type, or coverage considerations;
- the requested value is unacceptable and the network elects not to grant PSM rather than assign another value;
- the network does not accept the associated extended periodic TAU/PSM request.

The accept of the Attach or TAU procedure itself can still be successful; omission of T3324 is specifically a refusal/non-allocation of the requested active-time parameter, not necessarily a NAS procedure reject.

The UE must use the value actually signalled by the network. It must not assume that its requested T3324 was granted merely because it included it in the request. If no T3324 value is allocated in the accept, the UE does not obtain active-time operation from that request and does not start/use T3324 on the basis of its requested value alone.

The reference to **3GPP TS 24.008 clause 10.5.4.32** is a normative reuse of the **Supported Codec List IE coding and codec-bit semantics**.

In TS 24.301, the IE is carried in EPS NAS messages, but its internal contents are not redefined. Clause 10.5.4.32 of TS 24.008 defines, for example:

- the structure of the codec list;
- the system-identification context for the codec information;
- the codec bitmap/octet layout;
- the meaning of each codec-support bit; and
- any extension/length interpretation rules.

Therefore, an LTE/EPS NAS entity receiving a *Supported codec list* IE must decode the codec capabilities using the TS 24.008 definition, rather than treating it as an LTE-specific or arbitrary bitmap.

The reference does **not** import GSM/UMTS NAS procedures from TS 24.008, nor does it itself initiate codec negotiation or change EPS NAS state. TS 24.301 still specifies:

- in which EPS NAS messages the IE may or shall appear;
- under which conditions it is included;
- how the MME/UE uses the received capability information; and
- what happens if the IE is absent, malformed, or not applicable.

Its practical purpose is interoperability and consistency: the UE’s supported speech codec capabilities—particularly those relevant to interworking, CS fallback, and mobility toward GERAN/UTRAN—have one common encoding and meaning across 2G/3G and EPS NAS.

A UE enters **EMM-DEREGISTERED.ATTEMPTING-TO-ATTACH** when it is **not EPS-registered** and has started an EPS attach procedure.

This is not, by itself, a failure state. It means:

- the UE has selected a suitable/acceptable LTE cell and PLMN;
- the UE needs or is configured to obtain EPS service; and
- it has initiated attach, normally by sending an **ATTACH REQUEST** and starting the attach supervision procedure (e.g. T3410).

Typical reasons include:

- power-on / initial network registration;
- entering LTE coverage after PLMN search;
- a request for packet service from upper layers, such as mobile-originated data;
- re-attachment after an earlier detach, loss of EPS registration, or an attach failure for which retry is allowed;
- a change of PLMN or return to service after a period without suitable coverage.

The UE remains in this substate while awaiting the outcome of attach, including any allowed retransmissions of the ATTACH REQUEST.

Expected next outcomes are:

- **ATTACH ACCEPT**: UE moves to **EMM-REGISTERED** (normally `REGISTERED.NORMAL-SERVICE`);
- **ATTACH REJECT**, expiry of T3410/retry exhaustion, loss of cell, or an unsuitable PLMN: UE stops or aborts the attempt and moves to an appropriate deregistered substate, often **PLMN-SEARCH**, depending on the rejection cause and service availability.

Thus, the transition specifically indicates: **“the UE is currently deregistered, but is actively trying to become EPS registered.”**

The contents of the **ESM message container** shall be coded as a complete **plain ESM message**, i.e. using the ESM message format defined in TS 24.301 clause 9.3.2:

- the ESM header is present:
  - EPS bearer identity and ESM protocol discriminator;
  - procedure transaction identity (PTI);
  - ESM message type;
- followed by the message-specific ESM information elements.

The encapsulated ESM message does **not** carry its own NAS security header or separate NAS security protection. If protection is applicable, it is provided by the enclosing EMM NAS message that carries the ESM message container.

- When the UE sends a **REMOTE UE REPORT** message, it starts **T3493** to supervise receipt of the corresponding **REMOTE UE REPORT ACKNOWLEDGE** message.

- If a **REMOTE UE REPORT ACKNOWLEDGE** is received before T3493 expires, the UE stops T3493 and the remote UE report procedure is completed. No retransmission is made.

- On each expiry of T3493, the UE retransmits the outstanding **REMOTE UE REPORT** message and restarts T3493. The retransmission is for the same unacknowledged report procedure; it is not a new, independently initiated report.

- The UE performs this retransmission on the first through fourth expiries of T3493 — i.e. there can be up to **four retransmissions** after the original transmission.

- If T3493 expires a **fifth** time without reception of a **REMOTE UE REPORT ACKNOWLEDGE**, the UE does **not** retransmit the REMOTE UE REPORT again. It stops the retry process and aborts the remote UE report procedure.

Thus, the maximum transmission sequence for one report procedure is:

1. Original REMOTE UE REPORT;
2. retransmission after expiry 1;
3. retransmission after expiry 2;
4. retransmission after expiry 3;
5. retransmission after expiry 4;

and expiry 5 terminates the procedure without another REMOTE UE REPORT transmission.

After the final expiry of **T3493** (i.e. after any specified retransmission attempts have been exhausted), the UE:

- **aborts the Remote UE Report procedure**;
- **stops T3493** and clears the procedure/retransmission context;
- **indicates the failure of the Remote UE Report procedure to upper layers**.

There is no implicit EMM deregistration, detach, or other EMM state change caused by this failure. In particular, the UE must not assume that the MME received or acted on the Remote UE Report. A further report can only be initiated again when a new applicable upper-layer trigger/request occurs.

The **Protocol Configuration Options (PCO) IE** in TS 24.301 clause 8.3.16.2 is a NAS control-plane container for **external-network protocol configuration information** associated with a PDN connection/EPS bearer. It is not a vehicle for arbitrary user-plane or application payload.

It can convey, in either direction as applicable:

1. **PPP-related protocol configuration data**
   - In particular, PPP network-layer configuration information such as **IPCP** options.
   - The PCO coding retains the PPP-style protocol/configuration-option structure for such information.

2. **3GPP-defined IP/PDN configuration options**
   Typical examples include requests for, or delivery of:
   - IP address allocation information, including IPv4 allocation via NAS signalling where applicable;
   - DNS server addresses, for IPv4 and IPv6;
   - P-CSCF addresses, for IPv4 and IPv6;
   - MTU-related information;
   - IMS/IM CN subsystem related indications;
   - MSISDN and other subscription/service-related configuration values;
   - other 3GPP-defined PCO option TLVs applicable to the selected PDN type and service.

3. **Indications of the configuration mechanism to use**
   - For example, options related to IPv4 address allocation by NAS versus DHCPv4, where supported and requested.

Thus, the PCO carries **configuration requests and returned configuration values/protocol options** needed to set up or use the PDN connection. It does **not** carry ordinary IP packets, IMS SIP messages, DHCP exchanges as general user traffic, or arbitrary application data. The UE includes requested options in an ESM request such as PDN CONNECTIVITY REQUEST; the network returns the applicable values in the corresponding accept/bearer activation procedure.

A developer must consult **TS 24.008** because TS 24.301 does not define all mobile-identity encoding rules independently.

TS 24.301 specifies the EPS NAS procedures and tells the UE/MME **when** to use an identity—for example, whether an Attach, TAU, Service Request, or identity response should contain an IMSI, GUTI/S-TMSI, IMEI, or IMEISV. However, the underlying coding of the shared **Mobile Identity** format is inherited from, or explicitly references, TS 24.008.

In particular, TS 24.008 provides important common encoding details such as:

- mobile identity type indication;
- IMSI/IMEI/IMEISV digit encoding;
- BCD digit representation;
- odd/even digit indication and filler handling;
- TMSI-related coding conventions and field layout.

TS 24.301 defines the EPS-specific **EPS mobile identity** usage and extensions, notably identities such as the **GUTI** and its components (including MME identity and M‑TMSI), and may specify exceptions or additions to the 24.008 format.

Therefore, a correct implementation needs both specifications:

- **TS 24.301:** NAS state/procedure context, identity selection, EPS-specific identity forms and rules.
- **TS 24.008:** baseline Mobile Identity IE syntax and bit/octet-level encoding rules referenced by 24.301.

Without consulting TS 24.008, an implementation can choose the correct identity for a procedure but still encode or decode it incorrectly—for example by mishandling BCD digits, identity-type bits, odd/even parity, or unused-digit filler.

In TS 24.301, the **Mobile identity** information element is not independently redefined. Its coding is specified by normative reference to **3GPP TS 24.008, subclause 10.5.1.4**.

Thus, TS 24.008 provides the actual IE format and coding rules, including, as applicable:

- mobile-identity type indication;
- IMSI encoding;
- IMEI and IMEISV encoding;
- odd/even indication and BCD digit packing;
- the TMSI/P-TMSI form where that identity type is used.

TS 24.301 uses that IE in EPS NAS/EMM procedures—for example, where the network requests or the UE returns an IMSI, IMEI, or IMEISV—but determines from the particular message/procedure which identity types are valid or expected.

A related but important distinction is that **“EPS mobile identity”** in TS 24.301 is a separate IE from the TS 24.008-derived **“Mobile identity”** IE. The EPS mobile identity IE supports EPS-specific identity use, notably the **GUTI** (including MME Group ID, MME Code, and M-TMSI). A GUTI is therefore not obtained by interpreting the TS 24.008 Mobile identity IE as a full EPS identity; it is encoded using the TS 24.301 EPS mobile identity IE.

In short:

- **TS 24.008:** defines the base Mobile identity IE encoding.
- **TS 24.301:** reuses that IE for EPS NAS where applicable, constrains its use by procedure/message, and separately defines the EPS mobile identity IE for EPS identities such as GUTI.

A paging indication is normally a trigger for an idle UE to establish NAS/RRC connectivity (typically by starting the service request procedure) so that the network can deliver mobile-terminated signalling or user-plane data.

If the UE already has an ongoing **UE-initiated EMM specific procedure**—for example, it has initiated an attach or tracking area update and is awaiting the network response—it must ignore the paging indication because:

- the UE has already initiated the NAS signalling interaction needed to reach the network;
- it is in a procedure-specific EMM state, with associated retransmission timers and expected downlink NAS messages;
- starting another paging-triggered access/service request would create overlapping or conflicting EMM procedures and potentially inconsistent state at the UE and MME;
- the paging may have crossed with the UE’s uplink signalling or may relate to traffic that can be handled once the already-initiated connection/procedure completes.

In short, TS 24.301 serializes EMM procedures: a paging indication must not cause the UE to start a second access-related EMM procedure while its own EMM procedure is still in progress. The UE completes, fails, or otherwise resolves the ongoing UE-initiated procedure first; the network can then deliver the pending service or page again if necessary.

The detailed behavior descriptions in clause 5.2.1 are primarily intended to define the **UE-side operation of EMM procedures** in a consistent, implementable way.

They explain, for each procedure and condition, such matters as:

- when the UE initiates or responds to a procedure;
- which EMM state/substate and conditions apply;
- which NAS messages the UE sends or expects;
- how timers, retransmissions, failures, rejects, and abnormal cases are handled; and
- what state and context updates result.

Thus, their purpose is to make the required NAS procedural behavior—especially the UE behavior under normal and exceptional conditions—unambiguous, rather than merely describing the high-level service or independently specifying the network implementation.

TS 24.301 does not define a “USS address” as a Protocol Configuration Option (PCO) that a UE provides during EPS NAS procedures. Therefore, there is no 24.301-defined purpose or behaviour associated with a UE supplying such an address.

PCO is carried in ESM procedures such as **PDN CONNECTIVITY REQUEST**, **ACTIVATE DEFAULT EPS BEARER CONTEXT ACCEPT/REQUEST**, and related PDN/bearer procedures. Its purpose is to negotiate or transfer PDN-specific protocol parameters—for example:

- DNS server addresses;
- P-CSCF addresses or requests for them;
- IP-address allocation method;
- IMS signalling indication;
- MTU, DHCP, and other IP-layer configuration;
- APN-specific parameters.

Normally, the UE uses PCO to **request** configuration and the network returns the applicable configuration in the ESM response. It is not a generic mechanism for the UE to advertise an arbitrary server address to the network.

If “USS” was intended to mean **USSD**, that is different: USSD is handled by supplementary-service NAS procedures (e.g. TS 24.090), not by EPS PCO. If another feature-specific meaning of USS is intended, it would need to come from a different 3GPP specification or an operator/vendor extension, rather than the standard TS 24.301 PCO procedure.

If a network-initiated detach is still in progress and the MME receives a UE `DETACH REQUEST` whose detach type indicates **“switch off”**, the UE request takes precedence.

The network:

- aborts its outstanding network-initiated detach procedure (including stopping any retransmission/waiting for the UE response to the network `DETACH REQUEST`);
- handles the received message as a UE-initiated detach;
- completes the applicable network-side detach actions, e.g. releases EPS bearer/session resources and removes the UE’s registration/context as appropriate for the requested detach type.

Because the UE indicated **switch off**, the MME does **not** send a `DETACH ACCEPT`. A switched-off UE does not wait for, or receive, that response. Thus, the network does not continue waiting for completion of its own detach procedure; it regards the UE’s switch-off detach request as sufficient to proceed with deregistration and resource cleanup.

The **Detach type** information element tells the receiving entity **what kind of deregistration is being requested or commanded**, so it can perform the appropriate mobility-management cleanup and decide whether a new attach is required.

In particular, it indicates the scope and circumstances of the detach, such as:

- **EPS detach**: remove EPS registration only;
- **IMSI detach**: detach from non-EPS/legacy domain registration;
- **combined EPS/IMSI detach**: remove both EPS and non-EPS registrations;
- whether the UE is **switching off** (for UE-originated detach);
- for network-initiated detach, whether the UE is **required to re-attach** or not.

Thus, its primary function is to control how the UE and network release registration and bearer/context state, and whether subsequent re-registration is expected.

The UE shall **ignore the DEACTIVATE EPS BEARER CONTEXT REQUEST**.

An unassigned or reserved EPS bearer identity is not a valid identifier for an established EPS bearer context. Therefore, the UE shall:

- not deactivate any bearer context;
- not send a **DEACTIVATE EPS BEARER CONTEXT ACCEPT**;
- not alter ESM/EMM state or bearer-related state.

In effect, the message is discarded because its EPS bearer identity cannot identify an existing EPS bearer context.

The **Additional information requested** IE is a UE indication that it wants the network to run the **EMM information procedure** after the registration-related procedure succeeds.

The UE includes it in an **ATTACH REQUEST** or a **TRACKING AREA UPDATE REQUEST** when it requires current network-supplied EMM information—for example, information used for network presentation or time handling, such as:

- full and/or short network name;
- local time zone;
- universal time and local time zone;
- daylight-saving-time information.

A typical reason is that the UE has entered or registered on a network/PLMN for which it does not have valid current display/time information, or it otherwise wants that information refreshed.

This IE is therefore **not a general-purpose capability indication**, nor is it required in every attach or TAU. Its presence explicitly requests that the MME provide the relevant information, normally by sending an **EMM INFORMATION** message after successful completion of the attach or TAU procedure.

The dedicated **ESM and 5GSM coordination** clause exists because an interworking UE can be subject to both:

- **ESM** procedures for EPS/EPC PDN connections and EPS bearers; and
- **5GSM** procedures for 5GS PDU sessions.

Although these are separate NAS session-management protocols with separate state machines, they can concern the same user connectivity when the UE moves between EPS and 5GS, performs EPS fallback, has dual registration, or otherwise uses EPS–5GS interworking.

The clause therefore defines UE coordination rules so that ESM and 5GSM actions do not produce conflicting or inconsistent session state. In particular, it is intended to ensure that the UE:

- does not treat an EPS PDN connection and a related 5GS PDU session as unrelated, independently managed connections when interworking applies;
- avoids conflicting or inappropriate concurrent ESM and 5GSM procedure handling;
- preserves or correctly re-establishes connectivity during EPS/5GS mobility and fallback, where applicable;
- correctly releases, deactivates, or updates the corresponding local session/bearer state when one system’s procedure affects the other;
- avoids stale contexts, duplicate connectivity attempts, inconsistent IP/session state, or service interruption caused by uncoordinated NAS behavior.

Thus, the clause is not a new session-management protocol. It is a set of **cross-protocol UE behavior rules** ensuring that the EPS ESM state machine and the 5GS 5GSM state machine behave coherently in EPS–5GS interworking scenarios.

The network initiates a dedicated EPS bearer activation only in association with an already existing default EPS bearer context. A dedicated bearer is therefore linked to a default bearer; it is not activated as a standalone PDN connection.

The NAS procedure is initiated by the MME sending the UE an **ACTIVATE DEDICATED EPS BEARER CONTEXT REQUEST** message. The request identifies, among other things:

- the new dedicated EPS bearer identity (EBI);
- the **linked EPS bearer identity**, i.e. the default bearer to which the dedicated bearer belongs;
- the EPS bearer QoS for the dedicated bearer;
- the TFT, where applicable, to define which uplink/downlink packet flows use the bearer; and
- a network-assigned procedure transaction identity (PTI).

Typically, the request is triggered by a network-side bearer establishment decision, for example following PCC/PCRF policy control or a P-GW request to create a bearer for a particular service flow.

If the UE is in **EMM-IDLE**, the network first has to reach the UE, normally by paging and establishment of an ECM/RRC connection. Once NAS signalling connectivity is available, the MME delivers the activation request, commonly together with the access-stratum resources needed for the E-RAB.

On receiving a valid request, the UE creates the dedicated EPS bearer context and responds with **ACTIVATE DEDICATED EPS BEARER CONTEXT ACCEPT**. If it cannot accept the request—for example, because the linked default bearer does not exist or the requested parameters cannot be supported—it responds with **ACTIVATE DEDICATED EPS BEARER CONTEXT REJECT**, including an ESM cause.

The UE sends **ACTIVATE DEDICATED EPS BEARER CONTEXT ACCEPT** because the dedicated bearer activation is a **network-initiated ESM procedure** and the network needs an explicit NAS confirmation that the UE has successfully accepted and activated the requested dedicated EPS bearer context.

After receiving an **ACTIVATE DEDICATED EPS BEARER CONTEXT REQUEST**, the UE evaluates the request, including, for example:

- the dedicated EPS bearer identity;
- the linked default EPS bearer identity;
- the requested QoS parameters;
- the TFT and its packet filters;
- whether the UE can allocate/maintain the bearer and associated radio resources.

If the request is acceptable and the UE successfully activates the bearer context, it sends **ACTIVATE DEDICATED EPS BEARER CONTEXT ACCEPT**. This confirms to the MME, and consequently to the serving gateway/PDN gateway, that:

1. the UE has installed the dedicated bearer context;
2. the UE accepts the QoS and TFT associated with that bearer;
3. the UE can classify matching uplink IP packets onto that dedicated bearer; and
4. the network may treat the dedicated bearer activation as successfully completed and use it for the intended traffic flow.

The message is therefore not merely an acknowledgement of receipt. It is the UE’s confirmation of **successful activation** of the dedicated EPS bearer.

If the UE cannot accept the requested bearer—for example, because the linked bearer is not active, the TFT is invalid, the requested resources cannot be supported, or the requested QoS is unacceptable—it sends **ACTIVATE DEDICATED EPS BEARER CONTEXT REJECT** instead, with an appropriate ESM cause.

**EMM-SERVICE-REQUEST-INITIATED** is a temporary EMM state indicating that a UE which was already **EMM-REGISTERED** has started a **service request procedure** and that procedure has not yet completed.

It is entered in the context of obtaining/resuming NAS/RRC connectivity for service, for example:

- mobile-originated signalling or packet-data service;
- response to paging for mobile-terminated service;
- emergency service, where applicable;
- an extended service request, e.g. for CS fallback-related purposes.

Operationally, the UE enters this state when it initiates the service request and sends the relevant initial NAS request (typically a `SERVICE REQUEST`, or an `EXTENDED SERVICE REQUEST` where applicable) after establishment of the required lower-layer connection.

The state lasts while the UE awaits the outcome of that request—such as successful establishment of service/signalling connection, further NAS procedure handling, or rejection/failure. On successful completion it returns to the appropriate registered EMM condition; on rejection or failure, the resulting EMM state depends on the reject cause and associated EMM handling.

Thus, the state denotes an **ongoing service-request transaction**, not a separate registration status and not, by itself, an ECM/RRC connectivity state.

While in **EMM-DEREGISTERED.IMSI-DETACH-INITIATED**, the UE is in the process of deregistering from EPS and does **not retain normal EPS communication capability**.

It may communicate only as necessary to complete the detach procedure, i.e.:

- transmit or retransmit the **DETACH REQUEST**;
- receive and process the corresponding **DETACH ACCEPT** (if one is received);
- maintain the access needed for that detach signaling while the detach timer/procedure is running.

The UE does not use this substate for normal service:

- no normal user-plane packet service;
- no establishment of normal EPS bearers;
- no ordinary Service Request, Attach, or TAU procedure in parallel with the detach;
- it is not available for ordinary mobile-terminated services/paging.

Emergency service handling is an exception in the overall EMM model: if emergency bearer service is required, the UE can take the actions specified for emergency service rather than treating the pending normal detach as normal service availability.

The **MS network feature support IE is a UE capability indication**. Its bits must reflect the UE’s actual support for the corresponding EPS/S1-mode network features—not the current availability of those features in the visited network.

In particular, the UE includes/uses the IE to advertise support such as:

- **IMS VoPS**: set to `1` when the UE supports IMS voice over PS sessions in S1 mode;
- **EMC BS**: set to `1` when the UE supports emergency bearer services in S1 mode;
- any later-defined bits are likewise set according to UE support for the respective feature.

If a feature is not supported, its bit is set to `0`. Thus, the IE is not an indication that IMS voice or emergency service is currently usable; it is the UE’s declaration of capability, which the MME may use when deciding network feature support/authorization.

TS 24.301 clause 9.9.3.6 does not define a separate LTE/EPS daylight-saving-time meaning or coding. It specifies that the LTE NAS **Daylight saving time** information element is encoded according to 3GPP TS 24.008 clause 10.5.3.12.

Thus, TS 24.008 clause 10.5.3.12 supplies the actual value semantics:

- `00`: no daylight-saving-time adjustment;
- `01`: daylight-saving-time adjustment of +1 hour;
- `10`: daylight-saving-time adjustment of +2 hours;
- `11`: reserved.

Functionally, 24.301 reuses the legacy 24.008 DST IE definition so that, when the EPS network provides DST information (e.g., in an EMM INFORMATION message), the UE interprets the DST adjustment exactly as specified by 24.008. The LTE-specific clause governs its use in EPS NAS; the 24.008 clause governs its coding and meaning.

TS 24.301 defines the LTE NAS procedure and the messages in which daylight-saving information can appear, notably the **EMM INFORMATION** message. However, it does not fully redefine the daylight saving time information element itself.

Instead, TS 24.301 reuses the **Daylight Saving Time IE** specified in TS 24.008. Therefore an implementer must consult TS 24.008 to obtain the normative IE details, including:

- the IE encoding and length;
- the meaning of the DST value bits;
- the allowed adjustment values, e.g. no adjustment, +1 hour, or +2 hours;
- treatment of reserved/invalid values;
- its relationship to the local time zone / UTC-and-local-time-zone information.

In practical terms, 24.301 tells the implementation *when and in which LTE NAS message* the network may send DST information, while 24.008 tells it *how to decode and interpret the DST parameter*. Without consulting 24.008, an implementation could parse the EMM INFORMATION message structure correctly but still apply the daylight-saving offset incorrectly.

If the MME receives a **DETACH REQUEST** while an attach is still pending—i.e. after sending **ATTACH ACCEPT** but before receiving **ATTACH COMPLETE**—it shall treat this as termination of the ongoing attach.

The MME should:

- stop the attach-completion timer, **T3450**;
- abort the attach procedure; it must not continue waiting for, or retransmitting, the ATTACH ACCEPT;
- process the received DETACH REQUEST using the normal UE-initiated detach handling;
- remove/release the EPS bearer, PDN/session, and UE context resources that were established for the incomplete attach, as applicable;
- send **DETACH ACCEPT** when required by the detach type (normally for a non-switch-off detach); and
- release the NAS signalling connection and place the UE in the deregistered state.

Thus, receipt of the DETACH REQUEST takes precedence over completion of the attach; the network does not wait for ATTACH COMPLETE.

A UE enters **EMM-DEREGISTERED.ATTEMPTING-TO-ATTACH** when it has **initiated an EPS NAS attach procedure** while it is not currently EMM-registered.

In practical terms, this means:

- The UE is in an EMM-DEREGISTERED state (i.e. it has no valid EPS registration with the network); and
- The UE decides that it needs/wants EPS service and starts the NAS attach process, normally by establishing an RRC connection and sending an **ATTACH REQUEST**.

Typical reasons include:

- UE power-on, after selecting a suitable PLMN/cell, when it needs to register for EPS services;
- UE returning to coverage after being deregistered;
- UE having been detached, locally or by the network, and then attempting a new attach;
- UE losing its previous registration validity and needing to establish a new EPS registration;
- A request for EPS services requiring initial registration, including an EPS/IMSI attach as applicable.

This is a **NAS EMM state**, not an RRC “attach” state. It indicates that the UE is awaiting completion of the NAS attach procedure, including possible authentication, security mode control, and receipt of an **ATTACH ACCEPT** or **ATTACH REJECT**.

If the attach succeeds, the UE moves to an EMM-REGISTERED state. If it fails, is rejected, or cannot continue—for example because of timer expiry, PLMN restrictions, or lack of suitable service—it transitions to an appropriate EMM-DEREGISTERED substate such as PLMN-SEARCH or LIMITED-SERVICE.

A ProSe UE-to-network relay includes the **Remote UE Context Connected** IE to indicate to the EPC/MME that the service request is not only for the relay UE’s own traffic: the relay currently has a **Remote UE context connected through it**.

In particular, this is relevant when the relay is requesting NAS/RAN resources while it has an active remote UE using the relay for UE-to-network service. The indication allows the network to:

- recognize that the relay is serving one or more Remote UEs;
- associate the requested connected-mode/service resources with relay-assisted remote-UE service;
- maintain the appropriate remote-UE/relay context and route subsequent NAS or user-plane handling correctly.

It is therefore a **state indication**, not a generic ProSe capability indication or a request to discover a relay. It tells the network that a Remote UE is presently connected via that relay and that the relay’s NAS procedure must be handled accordingly.

In TS 24.301, the MME is the network-side controller and initiator of EMM common procedures. The UE does not normally initiate these procedures; it responds to MME requests/commands.

The MME uses common procedures as required in support of an EMM-specific procedure (e.g. attach, TAU, service request) or, where permitted, independently. In particular, the MME may initiate:

- **Identification**, to obtain or confirm the UE identity;
- **Authentication**, to authenticate the UE and establish authentication material;
- **NAS security mode control**, to activate/modify NAS security;
- **GUTI reallocation**, to assign a new temporary identity;
- **EMM information**, to provide network/operator information.

Thus, the MME decides whether such a procedure is needed, sends the initiating NAS message, supervises the UE response and timers, and determines the outcome for the associated EMM procedure. It must also respect the clause-specific applicability, sequencing, security, and collision restrictions—for example, authentication and security-mode control normally have to be completed before NAS messages requiring the relevant security protection are accepted or sent.

The reference to TS 24.007 gives the general transaction-identity rules that apply to the ESM Procedure Transaction Identity (PTI).

In ESM, the PTI is not a bearer identifier; it identifies a particular ESM procedure/transaction. Its purpose is to:

- correlate an ESM request with the corresponding response, accept, reject, or completion message;
- distinguish simultaneous or overlapping UE-initiated ESM procedures;
- allow the UE and network to determine which pending procedure a received ESM message belongs to;
- define how PTI values are allocated, retained while a procedure is outstanding, and released/reused, including reserved values.

Thus, TS 24.301 uses the PTI in ESM messages, while the reference to TS 24.007 avoids redefining the common NAS transaction-identity behavior. The PTI is especially important where several ESM operations may exist independently; the EPS bearer identity identifies the bearer context, whereas the PTI identifies the specific signaling transaction concerning it.

When the UE returns to **EMM-DEREGISTERED**, it does not restore the former EMM substate merely because it was previously registered on a PLMN.

If the UE has to select/reselect a PLMN—e.g. the previously selected PLMN is no longer usable, has become forbidden, or the PLMN-selection procedure requires another PLMN—the UE enters:

**EMM-DEREGISTERED.PLMN-SEARCH**

It then performs PLMN selection/reselection according to the PLMN-selection rules (including automatic/manual selection, forbidden-PLMN handling, equivalent PLMNs, etc.) and cell selection/reselection rules.

Once that process has produced a result, the EMM-DEREGISTERED substate reflects the resulting service availability:

- **EMM-DEREGISTERED.NORMAL-SERVICE**: a suitable cell of the selected PLMN is available and normal service can be obtained;
- **EMM-DEREGISTERED.LIMITED-SERVICE**: only limited service is available, such as on an acceptable but not suitable cell;
- **EMM-DEREGISTERED.NO-CELL-AVAILABLE**: no acceptable cell is available;
- **EMM-DEREGISTERED.ATTEMPTING-TO-ATTACH**: if an EPS attach is initiated.

Thus, the immediate substate for a required PLMN reselection is **PLMN-SEARCH**; the eventual substate is determined by the PLMN/cell-selection outcome and whether the UE proceeds with an attach.

For EMM cause **#42 – “severe network failure”**, the UE is to treat its saved EPS registration context as unreliable, rather than treating the PLMN or TA as forbidden.

The UE shall, in essence:

- stop the attach procedure (including stopping the attach timer, e.g. T3410);
- enter **EMM-DEREGISTERED** state;
- clear the stored EPS-related parameters associated with the prior registration/context. This includes, in particular, stale temporary/location/context information such as the **GUTI**, **TAI list**, last registered TAI, EPS update status/context, and associated EPS/NAS/bearer state as applicable;
- retain the permanent subscriber identity and subscription credentials, i.e. it is not an “IMSI invalid” condition;
- not add the PLMN or tracking area to a forbidden-PLMN or forbidden-TA list merely because cause #42 was received.

The UE should then perform PLMN selection and subsequently make a **fresh attach attempt** on a suitable selected PLMN, subject to the normal attach retry/back-off/timer rules. That new attach must not rely on the deleted old EPS context or old temporary identity.

If the **T3448 value IE** in the `SERVICE ACCEPT` indicates **zero** or **timer deactivated**, the UE shall treat T3448 as **not running** (equivalently, a zero value has an immediate-expiry effect).

Therefore, the UE:

- completes the control-plane Service Request procedure normally;
- does **not** apply a T3448 waiting restriction before another Service Request;
- may initiate a subsequent Service Request for further CP CIoT user-data transfer when needed, subject to the normal NAS/RRC and access restrictions.

It must not interpret a zero/deactivated T3448 as a reason to reject the `SERVICE ACCEPT`, deregister, or disable CP CIoT EPS optimization.

The MME shall process the request as an **IMSI detach**, not as an EPS detach merely because the UE is camped on a CSG cell for which it has no valid subscription.

The lack of CSG subscription prevents normal service in that CSG cell, but it does not remove the UE’s entitlement to emergency service. Therefore, where an emergency PDN connection/bearer is active:

- the MME performs the IMSI/non-EPS deregistration aspects of the detach;
- the MME sends the appropriate **DETACH ACCEPT** for the IMSI detach;
- the MME shall **retain the EPS emergency service context**, including the active emergency PDN connection and its emergency bearer(s);
- the MME shall not deactivate the emergency PDN connection or otherwise force an EPS detach solely due to the CSG-subscription restriction.

After the IMSI detach, the UE is effectively allowed to remain served only for emergency bearer services in that non-subscribed CSG cell. Normal EPS/CSG service must not be provided unless the UE becomes authorized, but the emergency connection is kept until it is released by the applicable normal emergency-bearer release, an explicit EPS/combined detach, or another relevant procedure.

A UE is in **5GMM-REGISTERED-INITIATED** because it has started a **5GMM registration procedure** by sending a **REGISTRATION REQUEST**, but that procedure has not yet completed.

This can occur for, for example:

- initial registration;
- mobility registration update;
- periodic registration update;
- registration after a network-requested registration update;
- a new registration caused by a change in registration conditions/configuration.

While in this state, the UE is awaiting NAS procedure progress or completion from the AMF. This is not limited to waiting for a `REGISTRATION ACCEPT`; it can receive intermediate registration-procedure messages such as:

- `IDENTITY REQUEST`;
- `AUTHENTICATION REQUEST`;
- `SECURITY MODE COMMAND`;
- `REGISTRATION ACCEPT`;
- `REGISTRATION REJECT`.

The UE remains in **5GMM-REGISTERED-INITIATED** while it responds to such intermediate messages and until the registration procedure succeeds or fails.

Typical outcomes are:

- On successful processing of `REGISTRATION ACCEPT`, the UE enters **5GMM-REGISTERED** (and sends `REGISTRATION COMPLETE` when required).
- On a `REGISTRATION REJECT`, or after registration failure handling, the UE generally moves to an appropriate **5GMM-DEREGISTERED** state, subject to the reject cause and any applicable back-off/retry restrictions.
- If the AMF does not respond, the UE uses the registration retransmission handling, including **T3510**, and retransmits or eventually aborts according to the specified retry rules.

Importantly, **5GMM-REGISTERED-INITIATED is a NAS mobility-management state, not an RRC state**. The UE may have an RRC connection released or need to re-establish access during the attempt; that does not by itself mean that the NAS registration procedure has completed.

The **Disaster return wait range IE** provides the UE with a randomized waiting-time range to use when it is required to return from disaster-related service/roaming to its normal PLMN service.

Its purpose is to:

- prevent many UEs affected by the same disaster from attempting return registration at the same instant;
- spread the resulting registration, mobility-management, and access signaling load over time;
- protect the recovering/returned network from congestion or overload.

The UE selects a wait time within the indicated range and delays its disaster-return related registration/return attempt accordingly. In other words, it is a network-controlled back-off/randomization mechanism for an orderly return after a disaster condition, rather than an immediate retry timer for an individual failed procedure.

Upon the **fifth expiry of T3581**, the UE shall treat the **UE-requested PDU session modification procedure as failed**.

The UE shall:

- **Abort the PDU session modification procedure**;
- **Stop T3581** and perform no further retransmissions of the `PDU SESSION MODIFICATION REQUEST`;
- **Return to the 5GSM-PDU SESSION ACTIVE state** for that PDU session, i.e. the pre-existing PDU session remains active rather than being released;
- **Notify upper layers** that the requested PDU session modification has failed.

The UE retransmits the `PDU SESSION MODIFICATION REQUEST` on the earlier T3581 expiries, but it must not retransmit it more than four times; therefore, the fifth expiry terminates the modification attempt.

NAS (Non-Access Stratum) is the 5GS control-plane signalling protocol layer between the UE and the 5G Core, with the AMF serving as the UE’s NAS-facing core-network function. Logically, NAS signalling is carried on the **N1 interface**.

Its role is to provide access-independent control procedures, including in particular:

- UE registration/deregistration and reachability;
- mobility and connection management;
- NAS security establishment and protection of NAS messages;
- authentication and identity handling;
- transport of PDU session management signalling, including signalling that the AMF relays toward the SMF;
- support for UE configuration and related control functions.

NAS is distinct from the Access Stratum (AS), such as RRC and radio bearer protocols. The RAN transports NAS messages transparently between the UE and AMF; it does not terminate or interpret the NAS protocol in the way the UE and AMF do. NAS security is therefore an end-to-end UE–AMF security context for NAS signalling, subject to the applicable procedure and message protection rules.

Architecturally, 5GS NAS is founded on two main protocol functions/sub-layers:

1. **5G Mobility Management (5GMM)** — handling registration, mobility, connection management, authentication, security, and related UE/AMF control procedures.
2. **5G Session Management (5GSM)** — handling PDU session establishment, modification, and release. Although NAS carries 5GSM messages via the AMF, the session-management control relationship can involve the SMF as the relevant session-control function.

Thus, NAS is the access-independent UE-to-core control-plane protocol framework, built around the 5GMM and 5GSM protocol functions and transported over the access network rather than being part of the radio-access protocol stack itself.

The NAS procedures are organized primarily by **NAS functional area**, rather than as one end-to-end call flow.

- **5G Mobility Management (5GMM) procedures** are specified in **clause 5**. These cover the UE–AMF control functions, including:
  - common/general NAS handling and NAS transport;
  - authentication, NAS security, and identity procedures;
  - registration and deregistration management;
  - connection management, including service request and paging-related behavior;
  - UE configuration update;
  - notification, status, and related supporting procedures.

- **5G Session Management (5GSM) procedures** are specified separately in **clause 6**. These cover PDU session control, notably:
  - PDU session establishment;
  - PDU session modification;
  - PDU session release; and
  - associated session-related handling, including the use of NAS transport between the UE and AMF/SMF.

Within each functional procedure, the specification normally defines:

1. the applicability and initiating conditions;
2. the UE and network/AMF actions;
3. the NAS messages and information elements used;
4. state-dependent behavior—for example, the relevant `5GMM-REGISTERED`, `5GMM-DEREGISTERED`, `5GMM-IDLE`, or `5GMM-CONNECTED` conditions;
5. timer, retransmission, error, collision, and abnormal-case handling.

Thus, registration, security, service request, configuration update, and PDU-session control are distinct procedures, but they interact through the 5GMM and 5GSM states and through NAS transport messages. The message formats themselves are specified separately in the message and information-element clauses, rather than repeated in each procedure description.

Serving PLMN rate control is a **NAS signalling-protection mechanism**, not a user-plane throughput control.

The serving PLMN provides a rate-control value to the UE so that the UE limits the rate of the NAS procedures/messages to which that control applies. The purpose is to prevent a single UE, or a large population of UEs, from generating excessive NAS signalling towards the serving network.

This is important because excessive NAS activity consumes resources in more than one network function, for example:

- radio-access signalling resources;
- AMF processing and UE context resources;
- interactions with SMF/other core functions caused by NAS requests;
- transport and signalling resources in visited and home-network scenarios.

Typical causes include faulty software, badly behaved applications, massive IoT deployments, repeated session-related requests, and deliberate signalling attacks. Without a UE-side rate limit, a UE can repeatedly initiate allowed NAS procedures even when it has no useful user-plane traffic to send.

The network implements and signals serving PLMN rate control because the acceptable rate is an **operator and load-dependent policy**. The serving PLMN can choose a suitable limit for its current capacity, deployment type, and service policy, rather than relying on a fixed UE implementation assumption.

Operationally:

- the serving PLMN signals the rate-control parameters to the UE;
- the UE keeps track of the applicable message/procedure rate for that serving PLMN;
- if the limit would be exceeded, the UE defers the affected NAS action until it is permitted again;
- normal procedure-specific exceptions, such as emergency handling where specified, are not to be blocked merely by applying the generic rate control.

Thus, serving PLMN rate control is a preventive, UE-assisted way to protect NAS and core-network resources from signalling overload while still allowing the network to admit legitimate requests at a controlled rate.

When **Serving PLMN rate control** is enabled (i.e. the UE has received a Serving PLMN rate control value from the serving PLMN), the UE is responsible for enforcing that limit itself.

In particular, the UE shall:

- retain and apply the received rate-control value;
- limit the number of **UE-initiated NAS signalling messages** sent to the serving PLMN to the configured maximum, measured over the specified rate-control period (the Serving PLMN rate control value is expressed as a number of NAS messages per 10-minute period);
- defer a new UE-initiated NAS signalling action if sending it would exceed that limit, until it is again permissible to send it;
- replace the stored limit when a new Serving PLMN rate control value is received.

The control is intended to restrain UE-originated signalling load; it does not prevent the UE from sending NAS messages required as a response to a network-initiated NAS procedure, nor does it prevent NAS signalling needed for emergency services.

In the **5GMM-NULL** state, the UE is effectively **unknown to the serving 5GS NAS/MM entity** in the sense that there is no valid 5GMM context for it.

That means the network does not have a usable UE-specific NAS context such as:

- a current 5G-GUTI or other valid temporary UE identity association;
- an active NAS security context;
- registration and allowed-area information;
- UE reachability/location information for paging;
- PDU session and related UE context associated with an active 5GMM registration.

Consequently, the network cannot treat the UE as registered, cannot page it, and cannot use an existing NAS security context to communicate with it. If the UE needs service, the network generally has to establish/re-establish knowledge of the UE through the registration procedure, including identity handling and, where required, authentication and NAS security setup.

This differs from the other 5GMM states:

- **5GMM-REGISTERED:** the network has an established 5GMM context and knows the UE sufficiently to manage registration, mobility, reachability/paging, NAS security, and service requests.
- **5GMM-DEREGISTERED:** the UE is not registered, but this does not necessarily mean that all UE-related information has disappeared immediately. Depending on the procedure and implementation, network-side information or context may still exist for some time. However, the UE is not considered registered or normally reachable as a registered UE.
- **5GMM-NULL:** represents the absence of a usable 5GMM context. It is therefore the strongest “no current NAS/MM knowledge of the UE” condition.

Strictly, “unknown” should be understood as **unknown in terms of a current serving 5GMM/NAS context**. The operator may still possess subscription data or historical records for the UE elsewhere in the network; 5GMM-NULL does not imply that the operator has no information about the subscriber at all.

If the UE verifies the AUTN and determines that the MAC is invalid, it treats the authentication vector as not authentic and does not proceed with AKA authentication.

For 5G AKA, the UE:

- does **not** calculate or return an authentication response (RES*/Authentication Response);
- sends a NAS **Authentication Failure** message to the network;
- sets the 5GMM cause to **“MAC failure”** (cause value 20);
- does **not** include an AUTS resynchronization parameter, since AUTS is used only for a synchronization/SQN failure, not for a MAC failure.

The authentication attempt is therefore failed/aborted from the UE perspective. The network may subsequently initiate another authentication procedure, but the UE must not accept or use the authentication vector whose AUTN MAC check failed.

The **Extended protocol configuration options (EPCO) IE** is a 5GSM transport container for **protocol-configuration data associated with a PDU session**. It is not a general-purpose user-data container.

A UE can use it to send one or more **protocol/container-ID encoded configuration items** to the network, principally:

- **Requests for network-provided IP-service parameters**, for example:
  - P-CSCF address information;
  - DNS server address information;
  - MSISDN information;
  - IPv4/IPv6-related configuration information, where applicable.

- **Link and PDU-session configuration requests/indications**, for example:
  - IPv4 link MTU or non-IP link MTU related information;
  - APN/DNN rate-control support;
  - reliable data service related requests;
  - other 3GPP-defined PDU-session configuration capabilities or status indications.

- **UE-provided 3GPP-specific configuration/status data**, such as:
  - 3GPP PS Data Off UE status;
  - S-NSSAI-related information where defined for EPCO use;
  - mapped EPS bearer-context-related information during interworking, where applicable.

- **Other protocol-specific configuration data identified by a standardized protocol/container ID**, including PPP/IP-layer control configuration where that protocol/container type is applicable.

Thus, the UE uses EPCO to convey **configuration requests, capability indications, status, and protocol control options** relevant to establishment or modification of a PDU session. The network returns the corresponding accepted configuration values—such as DNS or P-CSCF addresses—in EPCO/PCO as appropriate.

The exact allowed containers depend on the NAS procedure, PDU session type, and the particular 3GPP release; the UE must not use EPCO to carry arbitrary application payload or ordinary user-plane packets.

The network retransmits the **SECURITY MODE COMMAND four times** upon T3560 expiry.

If **T3560 expires a fifth time** without receiving a valid **SECURITY MODE COMPLETE** (or otherwise successfully completing the procedure), the network aborts the security mode control procedure.

So this is:

- 1 initial SECURITY MODE COMMAND transmission;
- up to 4 retransmissions following the first four T3560 expiries;
- abort on the 5th T3560 expiry.

For CIoT, the lower layers provide the **radio-access and broadcast-information input** that NAS needs in order to select an appropriate core-network type. They do not independently decide the UE’s NAS core-network registration.

In particular, lower layers perform/assist with:

- **PLMN and cell selection/reselection**;
- decoding the cell’s broadcast/system information;
- determining whether the selected access/cell/PLMN can provide the relevant CN type, e.g. **5GC/5GS versus EPC/EPS**, where applicable;
- reporting that availability/selection information to NAS.

NAS then uses this lower-layer indication together with NAS-level information—such as UE capabilities, CIoT support, subscription and policy restrictions, current registration state, and whether 5GS or EPS service is appropriate—to decide which CN type to attempt to register with.

Thus, lower layers are the source of the **availability and access-support indication** for CN selection. NAS remains responsible for the actual core-network selection behavior and for initiating the corresponding registration procedure. Lower layers also carry out any subsequent cell reselection or access establishment needed to reach the selected CN.

The **Forbidden TAI(s) IE** is used by the network to tell the UE that one or more Tracking Areas (identified by their TAIs) are not permitted for normal 5GS service/registration for that UE.

Its purpose is to allow the UE to:

- store the indicated TAI(s) in its forbidden-TA information;
- avoid initiating further normal registration attempts while located in those TAIs;
- avoid repeatedly selecting/attempting service in areas where access is known to be disallowed, e.g. because of tracking-area or roaming restrictions;
- search for and register in a suitable allowed TA instead.

Thus, the IE can provide a list of forbidden areas, rather than forcing the UE to learn them one rejection at a time. It is not merely an indication that the *current* registration attempt failed; it provides mobility-related restriction information that the UE applies when it subsequently enters or camps on the listed TAIs.

The restriction does not prevent emergency services behavior where emergency registration/service is permitted by the applicable 5GS rules.

5GSM is scoped to **PDU-session control**, rather than to general mobility or registration management. The procedures in the 5GSM clause cover the NAS signalling needed to manage the life cycle of a PDU session, including:

- **PDU session establishment**;
- **PDU session authentication**, where applicable;
- **PDU session modification**;
- **PDU session release**;
- the corresponding **UE-requested** establishment, modification, and release operations;
- **5GSM status/error handling** and other PDU-session-specific procedures defined for particular features or releases.

Thus, the scope is not limited to creation and deletion of a session. It also includes changes to an existing session—e.g. QoS rules/flows, traffic steering or routing-related parameters, session AMBR, SSC mode-related handling where applicable, and other authorized session parameters.

The procedures are generally **per PDU session**, identified by the PDU session ID, and may be initiated by either the UE or the network when the relevant procedure permits it. They are transported as 5GSM NAS messages, normally carried within 5GMM NAS transport, while 5GMM itself remains responsible for mobility-management functions such as registration, connection management, and service request handling.

Cause **#77, “wireline access area not allowed,”** means that the FN-CRG is not permitted to obtain 5GS service from its **current wireline access area (WAA)**.

A W-AGF acting as the NAS entity on behalf of an FN-CRG therefore treats this as a WAA-specific registration denial:

- it terminates/clears the FN-CRG’s 5GMM registration context and associated access-side resources as applicable;
- it does **not** continue to retry registration for that FN-CRG while the FN-CRG is in the same WAA;
- it can initiate registration again only after receiving an indication that the FN-CRG’s wireline access area has changed (or another applicable new-registration trigger occurs under the specification).

This is not handled as a generic PLMN-wide prohibition. The restriction is tied specifically to the current wireline access area. Since the W-AGF is acting on behalf of the FN-CRG, the W-AGF performs this handling locally rather than expecting the FN-CRG itself to process the NAS cause.

The **Extended rejected NSSAI IE** lets the AMF tell the UE that one or more S-NSSAIs are **not accepted for use**, and provide the associated **rejection cause(s)**.

Its purpose is to distinguish a **slice-specific rejection** from rejection of the overall registration. The AMF can accept the UE’s 5GS registration and provide an Allowed NSSAI, while at the same time informing the UE that certain requested/configured S-NSSAIs cannot be used.

The UE uses this information to:

- identify which S-NSSAIs were rejected;
- determine why they were rejected, e.g. not available in the current PLMN or current registration area;
- avoid selecting or requesting those rejected slices for PDU session establishment in the applicable context;
- adapt future Requested NSSAI, including potentially retrying after mobility if the cause is registration-area-specific.

Thus, the IE is not an authorization of slices and does not itself reject the UE’s registration. It is a mechanism for the AMF to provide explicit, per-S-NSSAI rejection feedback—using the extended rejection-information format where applicable—so that the UE can maintain correct NSSAI selection behavior.

For a UE-initiated de-registration with the de-registration type set to **“switch off”**, the UE must not resume a suspended RRC connection merely to transmit the NAS de-registration message.

Therefore, if lower layers indicate that the RRC connection is **suspended** (e.g. RRC inactive/suspended):

- the UE does **not** request RRC resume;
- the UE does **not** send the `DEREGISTRATION REQUEST` to the network;
- the UE proceeds with the local actions associated with switching off, including local deregistration/clearing of applicable NAS registration state, and powers down.

The network will subsequently handle the UE as unreachable and eventually perform its own implicit deregistration as applicable.

The **RQ timer value IE** provides the UE with a network-selected value for the **registration-queuing (RQ) timer**.

Its purpose is to control and spread UE registration signalling when the network needs to queue or defer registration-related activity, rather than having many UEs immediately re-attempt registration at the same time. In effect, it is a network-load/signalling-control parameter.

When the IE is received:

- the UE stores/applies the indicated RQ timer value for the relevant RQ handling;
- when the condition that requires registration queuing occurs, the UE starts the RQ timer;
- while that timer is running, the UE defers the applicable registration request/re-attempt;
- when it expires, the UE may proceed according to the normal 5GMM registration rules.

It is therefore **not a NAS message retransmission timer** and does not by itself change the UE’s 5GMM state. It supplies a timer value used when the specified registration-queuing condition applies. Emergency and other explicitly exempt procedures are handled according to their separate 5GMM rules.

The **Non-3GPP NW policies IE** is used by the 5G network to tell the UE its policy on whether the UE may access the 5GS through a **non-3GPP access network** (for example, WLAN via an N3IWF).

In particular, it carries the **N3AN policy**, i.e. whether use of non-3GPP access to the 5GS is allowed or not allowed for that UE in the serving PLMN context.

From the network’s perspective, its purpose is to:

- enforce operator/subscription policy for non-3GPP access;
- prevent the UE from attempting or continuing non-3GPP 5GS access when that access is not permitted;
- allow the network to control this independently of the UE’s mere ability to discover and use Wi-Fi or another non-3GPP radio access;
- update the UE’s stored behavior when the policy changes, e.g. through NAS signalling carrying the IE.

It is therefore an **access-permission/policy indication**, not a detailed Wi-Fi selection policy or a PDU-session traffic-steering rule. The network still performs normal authentication, authorization, and registration handling; the IE gives the UE the network’s policy so that the UE can avoid non-3GPP registration/access attempts that the serving network does not allow.

The purpose is to ensure that 5GS access through a 5G ProSe UE-to-network relay is granted only to a UE that has been authenticated by its home network, and to establish the corresponding 5G security context.

In particular, the AKA procedure:

- authenticates the UE and the 5G core network (normally the UE’s home network) mutually;
- verifies the UE’s subscription/credentials for 5GS access;
- derives the 5G key hierarchy used to establish NAS security, and, where applicable, access-stratum security;
- allows the network to associate the authenticated UE with the NAS connection carried through the relay.

For a **remote UE** using a ProSe UE-to-network relay, the relay is essentially a transport path for the remote UE’s NAS signalling. The remote UE’s authentication is with the network, not with the relay as a substitute for the network. Consequently, the relay must not obtain the remote UE’s NAS security keys merely because it forwards the signalling.

The relay UE itself also has to be registered and authenticated for its own 5GS connection. Its authorization to act as a UE-to-network relay is a separate matter from merely completing AKA: AKA establishes identity and security context, while relay-service authorization is controlled by the network’s ProSe subscription/policy information.

The network updates the UE’s stored operator-defined access-category information by sending the **Operator-defined access category definitions** IE in NAS downlink signalling, i.e. principally in a:

- **REGISTRATION ACCEPT**, or
- **CONFIGURATION UPDATE COMMAND**.

The IE is not a per-category “patch” instruction. Its contents provide the set of operator-defined access-category definitions that the UE is to store for the serving PLMN. On receipt of a valid IE, the UE replaces its previously stored operator-defined access-category definitions with the definitions carried in the IE.

Consequently, the network modifies the stored configuration by sending the **complete desired resulting set**:

- include a new definition to add an operator-defined category;
- include a category with changed contents to alter that category;
- omit a previously configured category from the replacement set to remove it;
- provide an empty set, where applicable, to remove all such stored definitions.

If the IE is not sent in a Configuration Update Command, that command does not update the UE’s stored operator-defined access-category definitions.

A **5GSM STATUS** message is sent when the UE or the network detects an error condition in a received 5GSM message for a PDU session, in particular when the received message cannot be processed in the current 5GSM context/state.

Typical triggering conditions include:

- the message is **not compatible with the current 5GSM protocol state**;
- the message refers to a **PDU session that does not exist** or has an invalid PDU session identity;
- the received message contains an error for which a 5GSM cause is to be reported, such as an invalid or unsupported procedure/message condition.

The 5GSM STATUS carries the applicable **5GSM cause** (for example, “message type not compatible with protocol state” or “PDU session does not exist”) to inform the peer of the detected error.

The mobility-history storage procedure is UE-wide and spans the UE’s mobility across **all RRC states**:

- **RRC_IDLE** — cells entered through cell selection/reselection;
- **RRC_INACTIVE** — cells entered through reselection while inactive;
- **RRC_CONNECTED** — cells entered, e.g., through handover or other connected-mode mobility.

Thus, it is not limited to a single connected-mode handover transaction. The UE accumulates the history of visited cells as it moves between cells, subject to the specification’s retention/list-size rules. The network may subsequently request/report that stored history when the relevant RRC signalling permits it.

When a UE-to-Network Relay UE receives an `sl-ConfigDedicatedNR` that configures PC5 Relay RLC channels, it prepares an `RRCReconfigurationSidelink` for the applicable Remote UE(s) to convey the corresponding PC5-relay configuration.

In particular, the relay UE:

- identifies the PC5 Relay RLC channel configuration(s) in the received `sl-ConfigDedicatedNR` that apply to the PC5 link toward a Remote UE;
- includes the relevant PC5 Relay RLC channel add/modify configuration(s) in the `RRCReconfigurationSidelink` sent to that Remote UE;
- includes release information for PC5 Relay RLC channels that are released by the received configuration, where applicable;
- does not treat the PC5 Relay RLC channel as usable for the relayed traffic at the Remote UE until the sidelink reconfiguration procedure has been completed for that Remote UE.

Thus, the gNB-provided `sl-ConfigDedicatedNR` is used by the relay to drive the PC5-side reconfiguration: the relay forwards the applicable RLC-channel setup, modification, or release to the Remote UE through `RRCReconfigurationSidelink`, so that both ends of the PC5 relay link use consistent RLC-channel configurations.

When the UE receives application-layer buffer-level information from upper layers, it uses that information to populate the `appLayerBufferLevelList` carried in `UEAssistanceInformation`.

In principle, the UE:

- creates one `AppLayerBufferLevelInfo` entry for each application-layer buffer-level indication that is to be reported;
- associates the reported buffer level with the corresponding `appLayerId`;
- sets the `appLayerBufferLevel` field from the buffer-level value supplied by upper layers, using the encoding/range defined for that RRC IE;
- reports the current/latest indicated value—i.e. it does not derive the value from RLC/MAC buffer occupancy or attempt to combine it with lower-layer buffer status.

The UE must ensure that the value put into the RRC field is representable by the ASN.1-defined range and semantics of `appLayerBufferLevel`. Thus, if the upper-layer value needs quantisation or is above the highest representable level, the UE uses the applicable RRC encoding rule, including the maximum/saturated representation where defined. It does not send an out-of-range integer.

The list is only included when the relevant application-layer buffer-level reporting is applicable/triggered and the UE has buffer-level information to report. The UE also observes the list-size and application-ID constraints of `AppLayerBufferLevelList`; it cannot encode more entries than the RRC IE permits.

The purpose is to enable and control the UE’s role as an **L2 UE-to-Network (U2N) relay**.

By providing relay-operation-related configuration, the network instructs an eligible UE how to support remote UE traffic over sidelink and forward that traffic toward/from the network over its Uu connection. In particular, the configuration is used to:

- enable the UE to operate as an L2 relay for one or more remote UEs;
- configure the sidelink/PC5 resources, bearers and associated parameters needed for the relay-facing radio link;
- configure the association between traffic received on PC5 and the applicable Uu-side transport/bearer handling;
- allow the relay UE to forward both user-plane traffic and, where applicable, control-plane/RRC-related traffic for the remote UE;
- let the network control relay availability, supported services, radio-resource use, and the conditions under which relay operation is permitted.

The relay UE is still a UE from the network’s perspective; it does not become a gNB. Its L2 relay function is to provide the forwarding path between the remote UE’s sidelink connection and the network-facing Uu connection. Configuration alone does not mean that traffic is immediately relayed: relay discovery/selection and establishment of the relevant PC5 and network-side connections must also occur.

The **SCGFailureInformation** procedure is the UE-to-Master Node (MN) reporting mechanism for a failure of the **Secondary Cell Group (SCG)** in an MR-DC configuration.

When the UE detects an SCG failure—for example, due to SCG radio link failure, an SCG random-access problem, or an SCG RLC maximum-retransmission condition—it reports this to the MN by sending an **SCGFailureInformation** message over the MCG-side signalling radio bearer (normally SRB1).

Its purposes are to:

- inform the MN that the UE can no longer reliably use the SCG;
- provide the failure cause/type and, where applicable, related measurement/failure information;
- allow the MN to decide the recovery action, such as SCG reconfiguration, SCG release, secondary-node change, or other MR-DC configuration changes;
- ensure the UE does not continue normal use of the failed SCG while it remains connected through the MCG.

Thus, it is not itself an SCG recovery procedure. It is the notification that enables the MN, which controls the overall MR-DC connection, to initiate the appropriate recovery or reconfiguration action.

In `RRC_CONNECTED`, the UE determines its RRC mobility measurements from the currently applicable **`MeasConfig`**. The network provides or updates this configuration, normally in `RRCReconfiguration` (and, where applicable, other RRC procedures carrying a measurement configuration).

The UE does not simply measure every detectable cell/frequency for RRC reporting purposes. It applies the configured measurement objects, reporting rules, identities, quantities, and measurement restrictions.

## 1. Measurement object: what to measure

A **measurement object** (`MeasObject`) tells the UE the target to be measured, for example:

- an NR carrier/frequency (`MeasObjectNR`);
- an E-UTRA carrier/frequency (`MeasObjectEUTRA`), where configured.

For an NR object, the configuration can indicate, among other things:

- the NR frequency and subcarrier spacing;
- whether measurements use **SSB** and/or **CSI-RS**;
- configured cells and their cell-specific offsets;
- cells/PCI ranges to exclude or blacklist;
- whether particular cells are allowed or preferred for the object;
- the quantity-configuration index to use.

Thus, the measurement object defines the frequency/RAT and the reference signals/cells relevant to the measurement.

## 2. Measurement identity: which object is associated with which reporting rule

The UE uses each configured **measurement identity** (`MeasId`) to associate:

- one `MeasObject`, and
- one `ReportConfig`.

Conceptually:

\[
\text{MeasId} \rightarrow (\text{MeasObject}, \text{ReportConfig})
\]

A measurement object may be associated with multiple report configurations through multiple measurement identities. For example, the same NR frequency can be measured for both:

- an event-triggered A3 report, and
- a periodic report.

A measurement object which is not associated with an active `MeasId` does not provide an RRC measurement-reporting configuration through that identity association.

## 3. Report configuration: when and what to report

The associated **report configuration** (`ReportConfigNR` or the applicable inter-RAT report configuration) determines how the UE uses the measurements. It specifies such matters as:

- event-triggered versus periodic reporting;
- the event conditions, such as A1, A2, A3, A4, A5, A6, B1, or B2, as applicable;
- thresholds, offsets, hysteresis, and time-to-trigger;
- report interval and report amount for periodic reporting;
- maximum number of cells to include;
- whether to report cell-level and/or beam-level results;
- the measurement quantities to include, such as RSRP, RSRQ, and SINR.

For event-triggered reporting, the UE evaluates the configured event using the applicable serving-cell and neighbour-cell measurement results. It sends a `MeasurementReport` only when the entry/leaving/reporting conditions of that report configuration are met.

## 4. Quantity configuration: how measurements are processed

`QuantityConfig` provides the measurement-processing rules, including relevant Layer-3 filtering configuration for quantities such as:

- SSB-RSRP, SSB-RSRQ, SSB-SINR;
- CSI-RS-RSRP, CSI-RS-RSRQ, CSI-RS-SINR;
- corresponding inter-RAT quantities where relevant.

The UE uses the configured filtering before evaluating event conditions and producing reported results. Therefore, event triggering is generally based on the applicable filtered measurement quantities, not merely on an instantaneous PHY sample.

## 5. Measurement gaps and feasibility

The UE performs configured measurements subject to the measurement opportunities available to it, including:

- configured **measurement gaps**, when needed;
- its RF/measurement capabilities;
- serving-cell activity and other applicable RRC/MAC/PHY constraints;
- availability of the configured SSB or CSI-RS measurement resources.

Measurement-gap configuration enables measurements that cannot be performed concurrently with serving-cell reception/transmission, especially for inter-frequency or inter-RAT measurements. Absence of a gap does not necessarily mean that no such measurement is possible; a capable UE may perform it without a gap. But the UE cannot be expected to make measurements that are infeasible under its capabilities and the configured opportunities.

## 6. `s-Measure` can limit inter-frequency/inter-RAT measurement activity

If `s-MeasureConfig` is configured, it can be used to control whether the UE needs to perform certain inter-frequency and/or inter-RAT measurements based on the serving-cell signal level.

In principle, when the serving-cell measurement is above the configured `s-Measure` threshold, the UE may not be required to perform the relevant non-serving-frequency measurements. When the serving-cell quality falls below that threshold, the UE performs those measurements as configured. The precise applicability depends on the configured measurement type and associated rules.

## 7. Configuration is maintained until changed or released

The UE maintains the measurement configuration in `RRC_CONNECTED` until it is modified or released. The network can use add/modify/release lists for:

- measurement objects;
- report configurations;
- measurement identities;
- quantity configurations;
- measurement gaps;
- `s-Measure` configuration.

Therefore, the UE determines the active measurement set from the resulting current configuration, not only from the most recently received individual list.

## Important distinction

This concerns **RRC measurement reporting for mobility and network control**. The UE may also perform other physical-layer measurements needed for functions such as beam management, link monitoring, radio link failure detection, beam failure recovery, or CSI processing. Those measurements are not necessarily identical to the measurements configured for `MeasurementReport` generation.

In short: in `RRC_CONNECTED`, the UE performs RRC measurements according to the active `MeasConfig`; `MeasObject` identifies what can be measured, `MeasId` associates it with a reporting rule, `ReportConfig` determines when and what is reported, and `QuantityConfig`, measurement-gap configuration, serving conditions, and UE capability determine how and when those measurements are practically performed.

For a **periodical CSI report** where exactly one reporting quantity is set to `true` in `reportQuantityRS-Indexes`, that enabled quantity is the **sorting quantity**.

Thus, the UE ranks the applicable RS resources/indexes using the corresponding Layer-1 measurement quantity:

- only `rsrp` set to `true` → sort by **L1-RSRP**;
- only `rsrq` set to `true` → sort by **L1-RSRQ**;
- only `sinr` set to `true` → sort by **L1-SINR**.

The RS indexes reported are therefore selected/ordered according to that single configured quantity, normally with the highest measured quality ranked first. There is no need for a priority rule or combination of quantities when only one is enabled.

The UE shall treat this as an **SCG reconfiguration failure**, not as a successful conditional reconfiguration and not as a configuration it may partially apply.

In (NG)EN-DC, when the RRCReconfiguration being applied was received on **SRB3** and the UE cannot comply with any part of it—including an RRCReconfiguration that is being executed from a `ConditionalReconfiguration`—the UE shall:

- regard execution of that conditional reconfiguration as failed;
- not send `RRCReconfigurationComplete` for that failed configuration;
- initiate the **SCG failure information procedure** toward the Master Node, using the surviving MCG signalling path (normally SRB1);
- report the failure as the applicable **SCG reconfiguration failure** cause (and include the relevant conditional-reconfiguration identity/failure information where applicable);
- perform the associated SCG-failure handling, i.e. stop relying on the inconsistent SCG configuration/release the SCG configuration as required by the SCG failure procedure.

The UE should therefore retain the MCG connection if it is still usable; failure of the conditional SRB3/SCG reconfiguration does not by itself mean that the UE silently accepts a partial configuration or necessarily triggers an MCG RRC re-establishment.

The network controls UE measurement reporting by sending a **measurement configuration** (`MeasConfig`) to the UE, normally in `RRCReconfiguration`. The UE does not autonomously report every measured cell or beam; it reports only according to the configured measurement identities and reporting rules.

The main control mechanism is:

\[
\text{MeasId} \rightarrow (\text{MeasObject},\ \text{ReportConfig})
\]

Each `measId` associates:

- a **measurement object**: *what* the UE shall measure; and
- a **report configuration**: *when, what, and how often* the UE shall report.

## 1. Control of what the UE measures

The network configures one or more measurement objects, for example `MeasObjectNR` or an inter-RAT measurement object. These identify such items as:

- NR carrier/frequency and applicable SSB/CSI-RS measurement configuration;
- cells, PCI lists, allowed-cell lists, or excluded/blacklisted cells;
- cell-specific offsets;
- reference signal type and related measurement details;
- possibly another RAT/frequency for inter-frequency or inter-RAT mobility.

The network may also configure measurement gaps (`measGapConfig`) when the UE needs time to measure frequencies or RATs not measurable concurrently with its serving carrier.

Further, quantity configuration controls measurement filtering, e.g. L3 filter coefficients for RSRP, RSRQ and SINR. Thus event evaluation is generally based on filtered measurement quantities, not necessarily an instantaneous radio sample.

## 2. Control of when the UE reports

The network configures a `ReportConfig`, such as `ReportConfigNR`. It can request:

- **Event-triggered reporting**, or
- **Periodic reporting**, or
- special reporting such as **CGI reporting**.

### Event-triggered reporting

For event reporting, the network configures conditions such as NR events A1 through A6, and inter-RAT events where applicable. Examples include:

- serving cell becomes better than a threshold;
- serving cell becomes worse than a threshold;
- neighbour becomes better than a threshold;
- neighbour becomes better than the serving cell by an offset;
- a secondary-cell-related event.

The network controls the trigger behavior through parameters including:

- threshold(s);
- cell/measurement offsets;
- hysteresis;
- `timeToTrigger`;
- trigger quantity, such as RSRP, RSRQ or SINR;
- whether the condition is evaluated for serving and/or neighbour cells.

The UE sends a `MeasurementReport` only after the configured event condition has remained satisfied for the configured `timeToTrigger`, subject to the measurement configuration.

For event reporting, the network also controls:

- `reportInterval`: spacing between subsequent reports;
- `reportAmount`: finite number of reports or effectively unlimited reporting;
- `reportOnLeave`: whether to report when the triggering condition ceases to be true;
- `maxReportCells`: maximum number of cells included in a report.

Thus, an event can cause one report, a limited sequence of reports, or continuing reports while the event remains valid.

### Periodic reporting

For periodic reporting, the network configures the reporting interval and the number of reports. The UE then sends reports periodically for the corresponding `measId`, rather than waiting for a radio event.

## 3. Control of what is included in a MeasurementReport

The report configuration also controls the report content, for example:

- cell measurement quantities to report, such as RSRP, RSRQ and/or SINR;
- maximum number of neighbour cells;
- SSB or CSI-RS based reporting;
- RS-index/beam measurement results;
- whether beam measurements are included;
- CGI-related information where a CGI report is configured.

Therefore, the network can request only cell-level results, or more detailed RS/beam-level results when needed.

## 4. Control of whether inter-frequency measurements are needed

The network can configure `s-Measure` behavior. This can limit when the UE is required to perform inter-frequency measurements, for example by making such measurements unnecessary while the serving-cell quality is sufficiently good. Together with measurement gaps and frequency-specific measurement objects, this controls both UE measurement effort and the availability of reportable results.

## 5. Starting, modifying, and stopping reporting

The network can dynamically control reporting by adding, modifying, or removing:

- measurement objects;
- report configurations;
- measurement identities (`measId`);
- measurement gaps;
- quantity/filter configurations.

Removing a `measId`, or removing/replacing its associated report configuration, stops the corresponding reporting behavior. A finite `reportAmount` also naturally terminates reporting after the configured number of reports.

## 6. UE behavior and RRC state

The UE sends the resulting `MeasurementReport` on the RRC signalling connection, normally on SRB1 while in `RRC_CONNECTED`. Measurements may also be used locally for cell selection/reselection in non-connected states, but this does not mean the UE sends normal connected-mode `MeasurementReport` messages to the network.

In summary, the network controls measurement reporting through `MeasConfig`: it selects the frequencies/cells/signals to measure, maps them to reporting rules using `measId`, defines event or periodic trigger conditions, controls report frequency and duration, and specifies the measurement results to include.

RRC is involved because measurement gaps are a **network-controlled interruption of normal serving-cell operation**. A UE may need time to retune its RF chain, synchronize to, and measure another NR frequency or another RAT (for example LTE), while it cannot simultaneously receive/transmit on the serving cell. The network therefore cannot leave this purely to UE implementation; it must explicitly configure when such interruptions are allowed.

In TS 38.331, measurement-gap configuration is part of the RRC measurement configuration framework (for example through `MeasGapConfig` in `MeasConfig`). RRC can configure parameters such as the gap pattern/length, repetition periodicity, timing offset, and associated timing adjustment. This tells the UE exactly which periods are measurement gaps.

The reason for RRC control is that, during a gap:

- the UE may be unable to receive scheduled downlink data/control on the serving cell;
- the UE may be unable to transmit uplink data/control;
- the gNB scheduler must avoid expecting normal UE availability during the gap;
- the gap timing must be coordinated with the serving-cell timing and with other configured functions.

A measurement gap is especially relevant when the UE does not have sufficient parallel RF capability to measure a target frequency/RAT while maintaining normal service on the serving frequency. A UE with more capable radio hardware may be able to perform some measurements without gaps, but RRC provides the standardized network mechanism when gaps are needed.

The broader functional context is **RRC-controlled radio resource management and mobility measurement**. RRC configures:

- what the UE is to measure (`MeasObject`);
- how and when results are reported (`ReportConfig`, `MeasId`);
- measurement quantities and filtering (`QuantityConfig`);
- and, where necessary, the time opportunities in which off-serving-frequency or inter-RAT measurements can be made (`MeasGapConfig`).

The resulting measurement reports are used by the network for functions such as inter-frequency mobility, inter-RAT mobility, handover preparation and execution, carrier/frequency management, and other RRM decisions. Thus, measurement gaps are not primarily a data-plane feature; they are an RRC mobility/RRM mechanism that balances the need to discover and assess target cells against continuity of service on the serving cell.

The UE does not remain connected to NR waiting for E-UTRA to appear. On reception of the `RRCRelease` carrying `voiceFallbackIndication`, it is released to `RRC_IDLE` and first attempts the intended E-UTRA selection/redirection for EPS fallback.

If no suitable E-UTRA cell can be selected, the UE falls back to normal NR cell-selection behavior:

- it performs/selects a suitable NR cell in accordance with the NR idle-mode cell-selection rules;
- if an acceptable NR cell is found, it camps on that NR cell in `RRC_IDLE`;
- any further attempt to establish RRC—for example, to continue/retry the pending voice-related NAS procedure—is made through the normal RRC connection-establishment procedure, as triggered by upper layers;
- if no suitable NR cell is found either, the UE remains out of service and continues normal cell-selection/search procedures.

In particular, this is not an RRC re-establishment case: failure to find E-UTRA after a voice-fallback release does not itself cause `RRCReestablishment`; the recovery is return to normal idle-mode NR selection.

In NR, **RRC controls the configuration of user-plane radio bearers**, but it does not itself carry the user payload. User data is carried on **Data Radio Bearers (DRBs)** through the PDCP/RLC/MAC/PHY stack. RRC establishes, modifies, suspends, re-establishes, and releases those DRBs.

## 1. Bearer types

RRC distinguishes:

- **Signalling Radio Bearers (SRBs):** carry RRC and NAS signalling.
  - SRB0 is used for initial common-control signalling.
  - SRB1 is established during RRC connection setup and is used for dedicated signalling.
  - SRB2 is normally added later for further dedicated signalling.
- **Data Radio Bearers (DRBs):** carry user-plane IP/Ethernet data associated with PDU sessions and QoS flows.

Thus, an RRC connection may exist with SRBs only; DRBs are added only when user-plane service is required.

## 2. When DRBs are established

Typically, the UE first completes:

1. Random access and initial RRC signalling;
2. `RRCSetup` / `RRCSetupComplete`, entering `RRC_CONNECTED`;
3. NAS registration and PDU session establishment;
4. AS security activation, normally through `SecurityModeCommand` / `SecurityModeComplete`.

When the network decides that user-plane resources are needed—for example, following a PDU Session Resource Setup request from the 5GC—the gNB configures DRBs, normally using an **`RRCReconfiguration`** message.

There is no separate “DRB setup” RRC message. DRB establishment is an outcome of applying the `RadioBearerConfig` and associated cell-group configuration carried by RRC reconfiguration.

The UE confirms successful processing with `RRCReconfigurationComplete`.

## 3. What RRC configures for a DRB

A DRB configuration identifies the bearer and connects the protocol layers needed to transport data. Key aspects include:

- **DRB identity** (`drb-Identity`);
- Association with the core-network service:
  - normally an **SDAP configuration**, including mapping between QoS Flows/QFIs and the DRB;
  - or, where applicable, an EPS bearer identity for interworking;
- **PDCP configuration**, such as:
  - PDCP sequence number size;
  - header compression configuration;
  - ciphering/integrity-related operation;
  - duplication, reordering, discard and recovery behavior where configured;
  - whether PDCP should be re-established or recovered after certain procedures;
- **RLC bearer configuration**, including AM, UM, or other applicable RLC behavior;
- **MAC logical-channel configuration**, including:
  - logical channel priority;
  - prioritized bit rate;
  - bucket size/duration;
  - logical channel group;
  - scheduling request-related behavior;
- Association with the relevant **cell group** and, where configured, multiple transmission legs or split-bearer operation.

Conceptually, the bearer path is:

`QoS Flow(s) ↔ SDAP ↔ PDCP ↔ RLC ↔ MAC ↔ PHY`

RRC configures that path; PDCP, RLC, MAC, and PHY then perform the actual packet transport.

## 4. Relationship between QoS flows and DRBs

In 5G, the 5GC provides QoS in terms of **QoS Flows**, identified by QFIs. The gNB determines how those flows are mapped to DRBs.

A DRB can carry:

- one QoS flow; or
- multiple QoS flows with compatible radio treatment.

SDAP performs the QoS-flow-to-DRB mapping. In downlink, the QFI can be indicated to the UE; in uplink, SDAP uses the configured mapping to select the appropriate DRB for outgoing user packets.

RRC therefore configures the radio-side realization of the QoS policy, while the scheduler and lower layers enforce the actual radio-resource treatment.

## 5. Addition, modification, and release

RRC can manage DRBs dynamically while the UE remains in `RRC_CONNECTED`.

### Add

The network includes a DRB in the appropriate add/modify list. The UE creates or configures the relevant PDCP entity, RLC bearer, logical channel, and SDAP association before using the bearer for traffic.

### Modify

The network may reconfigure an existing DRB, for example to change:

- QoS-flow mapping;
- RLC mode or parameters;
- PDCP parameters;
- logical-channel priority or bit-rate treatment;
- serving cell/cell-group association;
- duplication or split-bearer operation.

This permits adaptation to mobility, load, changing QoS requirements, dual connectivity, or PDU-session changes.

### Release

The network places the DRB identity in a release list. The UE stops using that bearer and releases the corresponding lower-layer resources as directed by the configuration.

A DRB may be released because:

- the PDU session or QoS flow is released;
- radio resources are no longer needed;
- the UE moves to another configuration;
- dual-connectivity resources are removed;
- the RRC connection is fully released.

Releasing a DRB is distinct from releasing the entire RRC connection: the UE can remain `RRC_CONNECTED` with SRBs and other DRBs still active.

## 6. Security handling

User-plane DRBs are normally protected by Access Stratum security. After security mode activation, PDCP applies the configured ciphering and, where applicable, integrity protection behavior.

RRC provides the configuration and triggering framework, while PDCP uses the active AS security keys and PDCP COUNT/state to protect packets. Security context continuity and PDCP state handling become particularly important during resume, handover, re-establishment, and dual-connectivity changes.

## 7. Behavior in RRC states

### `RRC_CONNECTED`

DRBs may be configured and actively carry user data. RRC can add, modify, or release them through dedicated signalling.

### `RRC_INACTIVE`

The UE does not normally have continuously active user-plane radio resources. With `RRCRelease` including suspend information, the UE may retain the relevant AS context, including information needed for efficient resume.

On `RRCResume`, the network can restore/reuse the suspended context and can send further RRC reconfiguration if the bearer setup or mapping must change.

### `RRC_IDLE`

There is no active dedicated radio bearer configuration for immediate user-plane transfer. A new connection/resume-related procedure is needed before DRB user data can again be sent over the radio interface.

## 8. Re-establishment and failure recovery

After radio link failure or certain other failures, the UE may perform RRC re-establishment. The outcome for DRBs depends on the received configuration and on bearer-specific settings, including whether PDCP is to be re-established or recovered.

RRC and PDCP configuration determine such matters as:

- whether PDCP sequence-number/count state is retained, recovered, or reset;
- whether retransmission/recovery mechanisms are used;
- whether RLC entities are re-established;
- whether the network must reconfigure the bearers after re-establishment.

The network may restore bearer operation through subsequent `RRCReconfiguration`, or it may release/recreate DRBs if continuity cannot be maintained.

## 9. Mobility and multi-connectivity

During handover or dual connectivity, RRC may move or reconfigure DRBs between cell groups, configure split bearers, or change the PDCP/RLC placement. This enables traffic to use the master cell group, secondary cell group, or more than one radio leg, depending on the configured architecture.

The DRB identity and QoS relationship can remain logically meaningful while the radio resources and lower-layer entities are changed.

## Summary

RRC manages user-data bearers by configuring **DRBs** and their associated SDAP, PDCP, RLC, MAC, and radio-resource parameters. DRBs are generally added or changed through `RRCReconfiguration`, are secured through the AS security/PDCP framework, map QoS flows to radio resources, and can be suspended, resumed, re-established, moved, or released as the UE state, QoS needs, mobility situation, and network resource conditions change.

The **UEInformationRequest procedure is network-initiated**. There is no UE-side event that automatically starts this procedure.

When the gNB wants UE-held diagnostic, measurement, or history information, it sends an **`UEInformationRequest`** RRC message to the UE on **DL-DCCH** while the UE is in **RRC_CONNECTED** (normally using SRB1). The request contains one or more request indications specifying the information wanted.

Typical information the network may request includes, depending on the configured/requested fields:

- **RLF report** — information related to a previous radio link failure and/or re-establishment;
- **RACH report** — random-access related information;
- **Logged measurement report** — MDT/logged measurements previously collected by the UE;
- **Connection establishment failure report**;
- **Mobility history information**.

The actual trigger is an **implementation/network decision**, for example:

- radio-link-failure or re-establishment troubleshooting;
- mobility robustness optimisation;
- MDT/trace collection;
- random-access or connection-establishment failure investigation;
- SON/OAM diagnostics.

Upon receiving `UEInformationRequest`, the UE checks the requested items and whether corresponding valid information is available. It then returns a **`UEInformationResponse`** containing the requested available reports. If a report is unavailable, expired, invalid, or not applicable, the UE does not provide that report as specified for that information type.

Therefore, the key point is: **the network triggers the procedure explicitly by sending `UEInformationRequest` to a connected UE; it is not automatically triggered by the UE detecting RLF, RACH failure, or another event.**

For a UE in **RRC_CONNECTED**, the network provides or updates the measurement configuration using an **`RRCReconfiguration`** message, via the optional **`measConfig`** IE.

`measConfig` can configure, modify, or release measurement-related entities, including for example:

- Measurement objects (`measObjectNR`, `measObjectEUTRA`, etc.), i.e. frequencies/cells to measure;
- Reporting configurations (`reportConfigNR`, `reportConfigInterRAT`), including event- or periodical-triggered reporting;
- Measurement identities (`measId`), which associate a measurement object with a reporting configuration;
- Quantity configuration, e.g. RSRP/RSRQ/SINR filtering and reporting quantities;
- Measurement gaps, if required;
- S-measure and speed-state related measurement behavior;
- Other measurement-control parameters applicable to the configured RATs.

The configuration is generally **delta-based**: the network may add, modify, or release only selected measurement objects, report configurations, or measurement identities. If the `measConfig` IE is absent in an `RRCReconfiguration`, the UE keeps its existing measurement configuration unless another applicable instruction changes it.

After receiving and applying the `RRCReconfiguration`, the UE performs the configured measurements and sends `MeasurementReport` messages when the relevant configured reporting criteria are met.

An `RRCResume` may also carry measurement configuration when a UE is resumed from `RRC_INACTIVE` into `RRC_CONNECTED`; however, for an already connected UE, the normal mechanism is `RRCReconfiguration`.

`gapSharingFR2 = setup` is not, by itself, a command to create or activate an FR2 measurement gap.

If the UE has no active FR2 measurement-gap-sharing configuration to which the indication can apply, the UE has no shared FR2 gap resource to use. Therefore:

- the UE does not generate a new FR2 measurement gap merely because `gapSharingFR2` is set to `setup`;
- it does not start sharing gaps, suspend serving-cell activity, or perform additional inter-frequency/inter-RAT measurements on that basis;
- the gap-sharing indication is effectively inapplicable until an FR2 gap-sharing configuration is active;
- any measurements continue according to other applicable measurement-gap configuration, measurement timing configuration, and UE measurement capabilities.

Thus, `gapSharingFR2` acts as an enable/use indication for an existing applicable FR2 gap-sharing configuration; it has no operative gap-sharing effect when such a configuration is absent.

After an RRC resume failure, the UE shall create/update `VarConnEstFailReport` for the failed resume attempt. The report contains, in particular:

- **`plmn-Identity`** — the PLMN associated with the cell on which the UE attempted the resume;
- **`failedPCellId`** — the physical cell ID of that failed PCell;
- **`measResultFailedCell`** — the available radio measurement result for the failed cell, e.g. NR RSRP/RSRQ/SINR as applicable;
- **`timeConnEstFail`** — the time information associated with the connection-establishment/resume failure;
- **`connEstFailCause`** — indicating that the failure was an **RRC resume failure**.

Release-specific extensions can add further information, but the above is the core failure-report content.

The `plmn-Identity` is not inferred from the RRC resume identity or from the old inactive AS context. It is obtained from the **PLMN information broadcast by the cell on which the UE sent the `RRCResumeRequest`**—i.e. the applicable PLMN identity in that cell’s SIB1 `plmn-IdentityList`, corresponding to the PLMN on which the UE is camped/performing the resume.

An **MCCH information change notification is only an indication that the currently stored MCCH information may no longer be valid**. It is not the updated MCCH content itself and normally does not identify which specific MCCH parameter or MBS session changed.

Therefore, after detecting the notification, the UE reacquires MCCH so that it can obtain the current multicast/broadcast control information and act on it correctly.

This is necessary because MCCH information can affect, for example:

- which MBS services/sessions are being transmitted;
- whether a service of interest was added, removed, started, stopped, or reconfigured;
- the mapping/configuration needed to receive the associated multicast traffic channel(s);
- scheduling and radio-resource-related information for MBS reception;
- identifiers and other control information used to determine whether the UE should receive a particular service.

Without reacquisition, the UE could continue using stale MCCH information. That could cause it to:

- try to decode traffic in no-longer-valid resources;
- miss a newly available or reconfigured service;
- continue monitoring/receiving a service that has been removed or changed; or
- apply an obsolete service-to-radio-resource mapping.

Thus, the notification is a synchronization trigger: it tells the UE to read MCCH again at the applicable MCCH acquisition opportunity, so the UE’s MBS service and reception configuration is aligned with the network’s current configuration.

If an `RRCReject` contains `waitTime`, the UE treats the RRC setup attempt as rejected and:

- starts (or restarts) **timer T302** with the value indicated by `waitTime`;
- returns/remains in **RRC_IDLE** as part of handling the failed RRC setup;
- while **T302 is running**, the UE does not initiate a new normal RRC connection establishment / `RRCSetupRequest` attempt.

Thus, `waitTime` is a network-provided retry back-off time, typically used for congestion control. It is not merely informational and does not keep the UE in a connected state.

When T302 expires, the UE may again initiate RRC setup if there is an upper-layer request and normal access conditions are satisfied (cell selection, access barring/UAC, etc.). Any explicitly specified exceptional access, such as emergency access where applicable, is handled according to the corresponding RRC access rules rather than as an ordinary retry.

If the UE receives an `RRCReject` in response to an `RRCResumeRequest` or `RRCResumeRequest1`, and that resume procedure was initiated for an RNA update, the UE treats the resume as failed and shall:

- stop `T319`;
- reset MAC;
- enter **RRC_IDLE** — i.e. it does **not** return to/remain in `RRC_INACTIVE` for this case; and
- perform cell selection according to the RRC idle-mode cell-selection procedure.

Any `waitTime`/related barring information carried in the `RRCReject` is also handled as specified (for example, by starting the applicable timer).

A UE uses the `sl-RxPool` broadcast in `SIB12` as the **cell-common NR sidelink discovery receive pool** when all of the following apply:

- The UE is **camped on the cell** and has acquired a valid `SIB12`;
- `sl-RxPool` is **present/configured** in that SIB12;
- The UE is **enabled/authorized by upper layers to perform NR sidelink discovery reception** and has an interest in monitoring discovery messages;
- The UE is using the **cell-common sidelink configuration**—in particular, this is the normal basis for discovery monitoring in `RRC_IDLE` or `RRC_INACTIVE`.

The UE then monitors only the time/frequency resources, numerology/BWP, and other pool parameters defined by that `sl-RxPool`, and applies its configured discovery identity/filtering when deciding whether a received sidelink message is relevant.

The presence of `sl-RxPool` does **not** mean that every UE continuously decodes all sidelink traffic in the pool. It is used for discovery monitoring only by UEs that are configured/interested in sidelink discovery. If an applicable dedicated sidelink/discovery receive-resource configuration is provided to an `RRC_CONNECTED` UE, that dedicated configuration governs the UE’s reception rather than treating the SIB12 pool as an unconditional receive pool.

An IAB-MT initiates the **IAB Other Information** procedure to convey IAB-node-specific information to its **IAB-donor-CU** when that information becomes available or changes and the donor CU needs it for IAB-node management.

The main reason is to report the IAB node’s **IAB-node address information** (for example, an address used to identify/reach the IAB node in the IAB transport/topology context). Typical situations include:

- the IAB node has obtained its address for the first time;
- the reported IAB-node address has changed;
- an IAB-node recovery, re-establishment, re-parenting, or transport/topology change has resulted in new reportable IAB information;
- the donor-CU has configured/requested such reporting.

The message is sent by the **MT function** because the MT is the RRC endpoint towards the donor-CU, while the information concerns the overall IAB node/DU side as well. It lets the donor-CU maintain the association between the IAB-MT, the IAB-DU, and the donor’s IAB topology/transport context.

It is therefore not a normal UE assistance or measurement report. It is an IAB-specific uplink RRC procedure, performed when the MT is able to communicate with the donor-CU—normally in `RRC_CONNECTED`—and there is reportable IAB other information to provide.

A UE initiates the **SCG failure information procedure** in an MR-DC/DC configuration when it determines that the **Secondary Cell Group (SCG) can no longer be reliably used**, while the Master Cell Group (MCG) connection is still available.

The purpose is to inform the **Master Node (MN)** that the secondary leg has failed, so that the MN can take recovery action—for example, release the SCG, reconfigure bearers, request recovery/re-addition of the SCG, or select another SN/SCG.

Typical triggering situations include an SCG failure being declared due to, for example:

- **SCG radio link failure**, such as expiry of the applicable `T310` after out-of-sync indications;
- a **random-access problem** on the SCG;
- **RLC maximum retransmission** failure associated with the SCG;
- failure during an **SCG change**, synchronization/reconfiguration, or other SCG establishment/reconfiguration-related failure, where applicable;
- other lower-layer indications that meet the specification’s SCG-failure criteria.

The UE reports the failure using **`SCGFailureInformation`**, normally over the still-working **MCG SRB** to the MN. The report can include the failure cause and SCG-related measurement information collected around the failure, allowing the network to diagnose the event.

Importantly, this is not triggered merely because SCG radio quality becomes poor. The UE reports only after the poor condition or another problem reaches a defined **failure declaration condition**. If the MCG has also failed, the UE cannot rely on the normal SCG-failure reporting path; it follows the MCG RLF/re-establishment behavior instead.

If upper layers abort the RRC connection establishment procedure because the NAS procedure was aborted, and the UE has not yet entered `RRC_CONNECTED`, the UE treats the establishment attempt as unsuccessful and abandons it.

In particular, the UE shall:

- stop the establishment timer `T300` (if running);
- reset MAC and release the MAC configuration associated with the attempted establishment;
- release any radio-bearer/lower-layer configuration created for the unsuccessful attempt, as applicable;
- discard the pending RRC connection-establishment context;
- transition/remain in `RRC_IDLE`;
- perform the applicable idle-mode cell-selection actions; and
- report the establishment failure/abort outcome to upper layers as applicable.

The UE does **not** wait for an `RRCSetup` after the NAS abort, and it does not send an `RRCRelease` to the network: an RRC connection was never established.

The UE does this to establish a **baseline** for change-based SS-RSRP reporting of the SpCell.

The “reference SS-RSRP” is an internal stored value, not a new radio measurement configuration and not a replacement for the measured SS-RSRP. By setting it to the **current** SS-RSRP of the SpCell, the UE starts the relevant monitoring/reporting procedure from the radio condition that exists at that moment.

Subsequent SpCell SS-RSRP measurements can then be compared against that reference value. If the configured SS-RSRP-change criterion is met—i.e. the change from the stored reference exceeds the configured threshold—the UE can perform the corresponding reporting/action and, where specified, refresh the reference value.

This is needed so that the UE:

- evaluates a **change** in serving-cell radio conditions, rather than an absolute SS-RSRP level;
- does not use an obsolete SS-RSRP value from before a configuration change, re-establishment, or prior reporting cycle;
- avoids immediately treating an already-existing low/high SS-RSRP as a newly occurred change;
- provides the network an indication when the SpCell’s radio quality has materially changed since the applicable reference point.

In short, the current SpCell SS-RSRP is stored as the reference so that later SS-RSRP variation can be measured relative to a valid, current baseline.

The **Counter Check procedure** allows the network to verify that the UE and gNB still have consistent **PDCP COUNT state** for selected DRBs.

PDCP COUNT, composed of the PDCP sequence number and higher-layer count bits, is security-relevant because it is used as an input to:

- user-plane ciphering;
- integrity protection where applicable; and
- PDCP replay protection.

In `RRC_CONNECTED`, typically after AS security has been established and when the network wants to validate state, the gNB can send `CounterCheck` for one or more DRBs. The UE returns a `CounterCheckResponse` containing its relevant COUNT-MSB information. The network compares the UE-reported values with its own PDCP state.

From a security perspective, this helps detect cases such as:

- UE/gNB PDCP state desynchronization;
- use of stale bearer state after mobility, recovery, or re-establishment-related situations;
- abnormal counter evolution potentially consistent with replay, loss/insertion of PDCP PDUs, or manipulation of bearer state;
- a situation in which ciphering could otherwise be applied with inconsistent COUNT inputs.

This is important because reusing, rolling back, or incorrectly synchronizing COUNT values can weaken the intended anti-replay and ciphering properties. For example, PDCP COUNT contributes to the ciphering input; a repeated or inconsistent COUNT under the same security context is undesirable and may indicate a security or state-management problem.

The procedure is not, by itself, a new authentication mechanism and does not magically identify every attacker. The CounterCheck messages are protected using the established AS security context, so an attacker without the keys should not be able to forge a valid response. Rather, the procedure gives the network a protected way to detect that the UE’s security-relevant PDCP state does not match the network’s expectation. On detecting a mismatch, the network can apply its policy, such as bearer recovery, re-establishment-related handling, release, or security-context/key management actions.

A frequency being an **MBS frequency of interest** is driven by the UE’s interest in an MBS service and by the service’s applicability/availability on that frequency or in its service area—not by the instantaneous existence of a broadcast MRB on that frequency.

A broadcast MRB is a current NG-RAN radio-resource realization of an MBS session. NG-RAN may not currently have established such an MRB on a frequency because, for example:

- no interested UE is currently present in the relevant cells;
- the MBS session is temporarily not being transmitted over radio there;
- NG-RAN has not yet activated the radio delivery for that session on that frequency;
- the session may currently be served by another delivery mode, such as unicast or another MBS radio configuration;
- the UE is anticipating reception after mobility/reselection into cells on that frequency.

The UE can nevertheless regard the frequency as of interest when it wants the relevant MBS service and knows, e.g. from service/session information, system information, configuration, or service-area information, that the service can be provided there.

This distinction is intentional: UE interest information can be used by NG-RAN to make the delivery decision. In particular, the network may establish or activate a broadcast MRB on that frequency in response to UE interest, rather than requiring the MRB to already exist before the UE can express that interest.

When the UE detects that either the `messageIdentifier` or the `serialNumber` received in SIB7 is different from the currently stored values for SIB7, it shall treat the received SIB7 as belonging to a new ETWS secondary notification/warning message.

Accordingly, the UE shall:

- discard the previously stored SIB7 warning-message information/segments associated with the old identifier and serial number;
- replace the current SIB7 `messageIdentifier` and `serialNumber` with the newly received values;
- start storing and assembling the warning-message segments for the new SIB7 message, beginning with the received segment.

The UE must not combine segments from the old and new `(messageIdentifier, serialNumber)` combinations. Once all segments of the new warning message have been received, it provides the reassembled warning message to upper layers.

Event X2 is an **event-triggered measurement-reporting condition for serving-cell deterioration**.

The UE monitors the configured serving-cell measurement quantity (for example, RSRP, RSRQ, or SINR). It considers Event X2 to be fulfilled when the serving-cell quality falls below the configured absolute X2 threshold, subject to the configured hysteresis and time-to-trigger. Conceptually:

\[
M_s + Hys < Thresh_{X2}
\]

where \(M_s\) is the serving-cell measurement result.

Its purpose is to notify the network that the UE’s current serving link has become poor, so the network can make a mobility or reconfiguration decision, such as preparing or initiating a change of serving cell/configuration.

Event X2:

- causes a **MeasurementReport** according to the applicable report configuration;
- is not itself a handover, reconfiguration, or radio-link-failure declaration;
- is distinct from RLF detection: it is an RRM/mobility quality indication, not a declaration that the connection has failed;
- may also be configured with reporting-on-leave behavior, so the UE can report when the X2 condition is no longer met.

The UE declares **Event X2** to have occurred when the configured X2 **entering condition** is satisfied for the configured **timeToTrigger**.

In principle, X2 is a two-part condition:

1. The relevant serving/reference cell quality becomes worse than the configured **X2 threshold 1**; and  
2. The relevant candidate/neighbor cell quality becomes better than the configured **X2 threshold 2**.

Using the usual measurement-event notation, the entering condition is evaluated from the applicable *filtered* measurement quantities as:

\[
M_s + Hys < Thresh_{X2,1}
\]

and

\[
M_n + O_{fn} + O_{cn} - Hys > Thresh_{X2,2}
\]

where:

- \(M_s\) is the measured quantity of the serving/reference cell;
- \(M_n\) is the measured quantity of the candidate/neighbor cell;
- \(Thresh_{X2,1}\) and \(Thresh_{X2,2}\) are the thresholds configured in the Event X2 configuration;
- \(Hys\) is the configured hysteresis;
- \(O_{fn}\) and \(O_{cn}\) are, where applicable, the frequency-specific and cell-specific offsets for the neighbor/candidate cell.

The UE starts the `timeToTrigger` timer when **both** inequalities become true. If either condition ceases to be true before the timer expires, the timer is stopped/reset. Event X2 is considered to have occurred only when both conditions remain fulfilled continuously for the configured `timeToTrigger`.

After that, the UE performs the corresponding event-triggered measurement reporting according to the associated `ReportConfigNR`—for example, subject to the configured report interval, report amount, and reporting-cell rules.

RRC is involved because sidelink discovery is not just a PHY/MAC transmission or reception task. It needs policy, authorization, and radio-resource configuration before the UE can announce discovery information or monitor for it.

In TS 38.331 terms, RRC provides the control-plane framework that tells the UE, for example:

- whether sidelink discovery is allowed/enabled for the UE;
- whether the UE is configured to perform announcement, monitoring, or both;
- which sidelink carrier/BWP and resource pool(s) are applicable;
- the time/frequency resources and associated parameters to use;
- the discovery-related identities, destination/service context, and filters relevant to monitoring;
- priority, QoS/policy restrictions, and any applicable timers or validity conditions;
- whether the configuration is network-provided, system-information based, or preconfigured.

The reason is that discovery activity must be coordinated with cellular resource management and operator policy. Without RRC control, a UE could transmit discovery announcements on inappropriate resources, monitor the wrong resources, or perform discovery when it is not authorized or when sidelink operation is not configured in that area.

RRC involvement is therefore mainly configuration and control:

- **RRC configures/enables** discovery announcement and/or monitoring.
- **MAC/PHY perform** the actual resource selection, transmission, reception, and decoding according to that RRC configuration.
- Higher layers use the discovered information to determine whether a relevant sidelink peer/service is available.

The applicable source of configuration depends on the UE’s RRC situation. In connected operation, the network can provide or modify dedicated sidelink/discovery configuration through RRC signaling. When no active dedicated RRC connection is available, the UE may rely on valid stored, broadcast, or preconfigured sidelink parameters, subject to the specification’s conditions.

Thus, RRC is involved to ensure that sidelink discovery is performed only under the configured conditions and using controlled radio resources; it does not itself carry out the physical discovery transmissions or receptions.

For Event A6, the UE reports when a **neighbour cell becomes sufficiently better than a configured/reference SCell**.

The Event A6 entry condition is evaluated using the configured trigger quantity (e.g. RSRP, RSRQ, or SINR), after the applicable L3 filtering and offsets have been applied.

Conceptually, the UE enters Event A6 when:

\[
M_n + O_{fn} + O_{cn} - Hys
>
M_s + O_{fs} + O_{cs} + Off_{A6}
\]

where:

- \(M_n\): measured quantity of the neighbour cell;
- \(M_s\): measured quantity of the relevant SCell;
- \(O_{fn}\), \(O_{fs}\): frequency-specific offsets for the neighbour and SCell;
- \(O_{cn}\), \(O_{cs}\): cell-specific offsets for the neighbour and SCell;
- \(Off_{A6}\): configured `a6-Offset`;
- \(Hys\): configured hysteresis.

Thus, a positive `a6-Offset` means that the neighbour must be better than the SCell by at least that offset, in addition to the effect of hysteresis.

The UE does not report merely because the inequality is momentarily true. It starts the configured `timeToTrigger` when the Event A6 entry condition becomes true. If the condition remains true continuously for the entire `timeToTrigger`, the UE initiates event-triggered measurement reporting for the corresponding `MeasId`.

After triggering:

- the UE sends measurement reports according to `reportInterval` and `reportAmount`;
- the report contains results according to the configured report quantities and cell/beam reporting rules;
- the gNB decides whether to add, remove, or change an SCell—the UE does not autonomously perform an SCell change because of A6.

If `reportOnLeave` is configured, the UE can also report when the event leaves. The leave condition uses hysteresis in the opposite direction, conceptually:

\[
M_n + O_{fn} + O_{cn} + Hys
<
M_s + O_{fs} + O_{cs} + Off_{A6}
\]

Event A6 is therefore a **relative neighbour-versus-SCell event**, unlike A4, which is an absolute neighbour threshold event, or A3, which compares a neighbour with the SpCell.

An NR sidelink measurement object defines the **measurement target** for sidelink operation: i.e., what sidelink carrier/reference signals the UE is to observe and measure.

In practical terms, it provides information such as the relevant sidelink frequency and the sidelink reference/synchronization signals to measure (for example SL-SSB and, where applicable, sidelink CSI-RS), together with associated radio parameters such as subcarrier spacing or signal/index restrictions.

Its role is to let the UE obtain sidelink radio-quality information, such as sidelink RSRP-type measurements, for purposes such as:

- selecting or reselecting a sidelink synchronization source;
- evaluating candidate sidelink peers, relays, or coverage/synchronization conditions;
- monitoring sidelink link quality;
- producing measurement reports when the corresponding report configuration requires it.

The measurement object itself defines **what to measure**, not **when to report it**. Reporting criteria, thresholds, events, periodicity, and the association of a measurement object with a report configuration are controlled through the related measurement-report configuration and measurement identity.

Also, configuring a sidelink measurement object does not itself allocate PSCCH/PSSCH resources, establish a PC5 connection, or grant sidelink transmission resources. It is a measurement definition used by the relevant sidelink measurement and mobility/selection procedures.

An RRC_CONNECTED UE may need to apply a new network-provided sidelink configuration because sidelink operation is subject to the serving network’s current radio-resource policy.

In NR sidelink, the network can use an RRC reconfiguration to add, modify, or release dedicated sidelink configuration for a UE. This is needed, for example, when the network must:

- enable sidelink for a UE that has indicated sidelink interest, traffic, destination information, or QoS requirements;
- provide or change the sidelink carrier, sidelink BWP, resource pools, or related physical-layer parameters;
- configure or revise resources for network-scheduled sidelink operation (Mode 1), including configured-grant-related operation where applicable;
- change the resource pools and restrictions within which a UE performs autonomous resource selection (Mode 2);
- adapt sidelink resources because of load, interference, congestion, coverage, priority traffic, or operator policy;
- update the configuration after mobility, serving-cell change, BWP/frequency changes, or a change in the UE’s sidelink service requirements;
- release old sidelink resources/configuration that must no longer be used.

Being in `RRC_CONNECTED` is important because the UE has a dedicated RRC signaling connection and the gNB can therefore provide UE-specific sidelink configuration dynamically, rather than relying only on common or preconfigured sidelink information.

The UE must not continue to use stale dedicated sidelink resources after the network has replaced or released them. Applying the new configuration ensures that its PSCCH/PSSCH transmission, reception, sensing, and resource selection/scheduling behavior remains aligned with the network’s current configuration.

This does **not** mean that every RRC_CONNECTED UE automatically transmits sidelink traffic. The new RRC sidelink configuration establishes or updates the radio conditions and permissions for sidelink; actual sidelink transmission still depends on the configured sidelink procedure, available resources, traffic, and applicable higher-layer conditions.

For a `BH-RLC-ChannelConfig` whose `bh-RLC-ChannelIdentity` is **not already configured in that cell group**, the IAB-node treats it as an **addition of a new BH RLC channel**, not as a modification or re-establishment of an existing one.

It shall:

- create/configure the BH RLC channel identified by that `bh-RLC-ChannelIdentity`;
- establish the corresponding RLC entity;
- apply the included `rlc-Config` to that RLC entity, if present;
- configure the associated MAC logical channel according to the included `logicalChannelConfig`, if present.

`reestablishRLC`, when present, is relevant to re-establishing an already existing RLC entity. For an identity not previously configured in the same cell group, the required behavior is to establish the new BH RLC channel/RLC entity and apply its supplied configuration.

An **MBS Interest Indication is not a request for access to an MBS service**, nor is it limited to the case where the service is unavailable to the UE.

A UE can already be able to receive an MBS service—for example, because the service is being broadcast in the serving cell—and still indicate its interest because the network may not otherwise know that this particular UE wants that service.

The indication gives the NG-RAN UE-specific demand information that it can use for such purposes as:

- deciding whether to start, retain, expand, or stop MBS transmission/resource allocation;
- selecting or adapting MBS delivery, e.g. broadcast versus multicast-oriented delivery/configuration;
- configuring or maintaining MBS-related radio resources for the UE;
- supporting mobility/service continuity, so that the network can consider the UE’s MBS interest when the UE moves or is reconfigured;
- collecting interest/load information for admission, resource, and coverage decisions.

Thus, “the UE can receive the service now” and “the network knows the UE is interested in the service” are different facts. Reception availability may be cell-wide and non-UE-specific, whereas the MBS Interest Indication explicitly reports the UE’s current upper-layer interest. Subject to the applicable RRC configuration and triggering conditions, the UE may therefore send it even while MBS reception is already possible or ongoing.

A UE in **RRC_IDLE with no serving cell** can perform NR sidelink operation in an **out-of-coverage (OoC)** scenario, provided that it has an applicable **NR sidelink preconfiguration**.

That preconfiguration is typically provisioned by upper layers (for example, pre-installed/provisioned or obtained while previously in coverage) and includes the sidelink carrier/resource-pool and related SL parameters needed for autonomous PC5 operation. The UE can then use the preconfigured sidelink resources, including autonomous resource selection where applicable, without requiring an RRC connection or current serving cell.

Thus, the key case is:

- UE is in `RRC_IDLE`;
- UE has **no serving cell** / is out of network coverage;
- UE has valid **SL preconfiguration** for NR sidelink;
- the UE performs PC5 sidelink communication using those preconfigured resources, subject to sidelink synchronization and resource-selection rules.

Without such preconfiguration, lack of a serving cell alone does not enable sidelink operation.

A `measObjectId` is not self-contained: it is an identifier used by a `MeasId` to bind a measurement object to a reporting configuration.

In TS 38.331, the UE treats the measurement configuration as a delta to its stored measurement configuration:

- A measurement object may already be stored from an earlier configuration, or may be created/modified by `measObjectToAddModList` in the received `MeasConfig`.
- A `MeasId` in `measIdToAddModList` is usable only if its referenced `measObjectId` resolves to a configured measurement object (and its `reportConfigId` resolves to a configured report configuration).

If the `measObjectId` referenced by a `MeasId` does not exist after applying the relevant measurement-object configuration, the UE considers that `MeasId` invalid/ignores it. Consequently:

- the UE does not perform measurements for that measurement identity;
- the UE does not evaluate measurement events or periodic reporting for it;
- the UE does not send measurement reports using that `measId`;
- valid measurement objects, report configurations, and other valid measurement identities in the same `MeasConfig` can still be processed.

This is not normally an ASN.1 decoding error—the ASN.1 type only constrains the numerical range of `measObjectId`; it cannot enforce that the referenced object exists. It is a semantic cross-reference error handled by making the affected measurement identity unusable, rather than by treating the identifier as an implicit measurement object or reporting against an arbitrary object.

The inclusion of `ue-TxTEG-AssociationList` is governed by the **UE positioning assistance information procedure** in TS 38.331.

In particular, the UE includes this list only when it is providing UE positioning assistance information in response to the network’s relevant positioning configuration/request for UE Tx timing-error-group (TxTEG) association reporting. The list reports the UE’s association of configured positioning SRS resources with UE TxTEG IDs.

Thus:

- it is a **positioning-assistance-information report**, not a normal measurement report or a generic `UEAssistanceInformation` indication;
- the UE includes it only if the network has requested/configured the TxTEG-association information and the UE has the applicable association information for the configured positioning resources;
- absent that request/configuration, the UE does not include `ue-TxTEG-AssociationList` merely because it is sending other positioning assistance information.

When an L2 U2N Relay UE receives a configuration containing `uu-Relay-RLC-ChannelToReleaseList`, it treats the list as a per-channel removal instruction.

For each `Uu-Relay-RLC-ChannelID` in the list, the relay UE:

- identifies the corresponding configured Uu-Relay RLC channel;
- releases the RLC entity/configuration associated with that channel identity;
- stops using that channel for relayed traffic on Uu, including its associated logical-channel and RLC handling;
- discards/removes the channel-specific RLC state and buffered data as part of RLC-entity release, rather than attempting to continue or re-establish traffic on that channel.

The release is limited to the identified **Uu-Relay-RLC channel(s)**. It is not, by itself:

- an RRC connection release;
- a release of all relay functionality;
- necessarily a release of unrelated Uu-Relay RLC channels or PC5-side resources.

If a channel identity is released and also provided in the add/modify configuration in the same reconfiguration, the normal release/add-or-modify processing permits the channel to be removed and then configured again according to the new parameters. The relay UE applies the resulting configuration and, where applicable, acknowledges the enclosing RRC reconfiguration with the normal RRC completion procedure; there is no separate RRC response specifically for each released Uu-Relay-RLC channel.

The network enables a UE to act as a **U2N Relay UE** by providing it with a dedicated sidelink relay configuration through RRC signaling.

In practice, the serving gNB sends an **`RRCReconfiguration`** to a UE in `RRC_CONNECTED`, carrying the NR sidelink dedicated configuration (`sl-ConfigDedicatedNR`). For Rel-17 U2N relay operation, this configuration includes **`sl-RelayUE-Config-r17`**.

When `sl-RelayUE-Config-r17` is configured, the UE is configured to operate as a Relay UE for the indicated relay service(s), identified by the configured relay service code(s). The configuration provides the relay-related sidelink settings needed for the UE to:

- advertise/discover its availability as a relay on PC5, as applicable;
- establish and maintain PC5 sidelink communication with Remote UE(s);
- receive traffic from Remote UE(s) over PC5;
- relay that traffic toward the network through its Uu connection, and relay downlink traffic from the network back to the Remote UE(s);
- use the configured sidelink radio resources and bearer/QoS-related configuration associated with the relay operation.

Thus, being sidelink-capable or having generic sidelink configuration alone does not make a UE a U2N Relay UE. The UE becomes network-authorized/configured for U2N relay operation when the network supplies the relay-specific configuration—particularly `sl-RelayUE-Config-r17`—via dedicated RRC reconfiguration. The network can subsequently modify or remove that configuration to change or stop the UE’s relay role.

For every `drb-Identity` contained in `drb-ToReleaseList` that exists in the UE’s current radio-bearer configuration, the UE releases that DRB and its associated lower-layer resources. In particular, it shall release:

- the DRB identified by the `drb-Identity`;
- the associated PDCP entity;
- the associated RLC entity or entities, where applicable;
- the associated MAC logical channel(s), where applicable; and
- the associated SDAP entity, where applicable.

A listed DRB identity that is not part of the current configuration does not result in release of an existing bearer/resource. The release is applied as part of processing the received radio-bearer configuration; it is not a separate per-DRB RRC signalling transaction.

In the TS 38.331 architecture, the UE RRC layer is the Access Stratum (AS) control-plane protocol that sits between NAS and the radio protocol stack. It does not carry normal user-plane traffic; instead, it establishes, configures, reconfigures, suspends/resumes, and releases the radio resources used by the other layers.

The interaction is logical/functional: TS 38.331 specifies RRC procedures and the resulting actions on other layers, rather than requiring a particular internal UE software API.

## 1. Interaction with NAS

RRC provides the radio-access control function used by NAS.

Key interactions include:

- **NAS-triggered access:** NAS can cause the UE to initiate RRC connection establishment or resume, for example for mobile-originated signalling or data.
- **NAS transport:** RRC transports NAS PDUs transparently between the UE NAS and the gNB:
  - Uplink, typically using `ULInformationTransfer`;
  - Downlink, typically using `DLInformationTransfer`.
- **Paging:** RRC receives paging from lower layers and processes the UE identity and paging cause. When appropriate, this results in NAS being informed so that the UE can perform the relevant service or mobility procedure.
- **State support:** RRC radio states support NAS operation:
  - In `RRC_IDLE`, the UE performs cell selection/reselection and monitors paging/system information.
  - In `RRC_INACTIVE`, the UE retains an RRC context in the network and can resume more efficiently.
  - In `RRC_CONNECTED`, RRC maintains dedicated radio resources and enables NAS signalling and user-plane bearers.

RRC does not interpret NAS protocol contents for mobility-management or session-management purposes. It carries NAS information and provides the radio connection needed for NAS procedures.

## 2. Interaction with PDCP

RRC signalling is normally transferred over **Signalling Radio Bearers (SRBs)**, for which PDCP provides functions such as:

- RRC PDU transfer;
- ciphering and integrity protection once AS security is activated;
- PDCP sequence-number handling and retransmission-related functions, where applicable.

RRC configures PDCP through RRC configuration procedures. For example, an RRC reconfiguration can establish or modify PDCP entities and configure parameters for SRBs and DRBs.

RRC also controls AS security activation. After receiving the relevant security configuration from the network, RRC causes the lower layers, notably PDCP, to apply ciphering and integrity protection using the derived AS keys.

Typical bearer usage is:

- **SRB0:** RRC messages on CCCH, used for initial access before dedicated signalling is available;
- **SRB1/SRB2** (and, where applicable, other SRBs): dedicated RRC and NAS-related signalling on DCCH;
- **DRBs:** user-plane bearers. RRC configures them, but user-plane packets normally pass through SDAP/PDCP/RLC/MAC/PHY without RRC processing each packet.

## 3. Interaction with RLC

RRC configures RLC entities associated with SRBs and DRBs. It can instruct the UE to establish, modify, re-establish, or release RLC entities and configure parameters such as:

- RLC mode, for example AM or UM;
- sequence-number size;
- retransmission and status-report related parameters;
- reassembly and reordering parameters.

RRC PDUs are delivered to and from RLC via the relevant logical channel. RLC provides segmentation, reassembly, and, in AM, acknowledged-mode reliability for the RRC signalling bearers that use it.

## 4. Interaction with MAC

RRC configures MAC behavior and provides the control information needed for MAC operation. This includes, for example:

- logical-channel configuration and priorities;
- scheduling-request configuration;
- buffer-status reporting configuration;
- random-access configuration;
- discontinuous reception (DRX) configuration;
- configured grants and other uplink-related configuration;
- cell-group and serving-cell related MAC configuration.

MAC is responsible for multiplexing logical channels into transport blocks, handling scheduling-related operation, random access support, HARQ, and transport to/from PHY.

For initial RRC access, MAC/PHY provide the random-access and common-channel transport needed for messages such as an RRC setup or resume request. RRC determines which procedure is required; MAC executes the configured radio-access mechanisms.

## 5. Interaction with PHY

RRC configures the physical-layer operation required for communication with the serving cell(s). This includes configuration related to:

- serving cells and bandwidth parts;
- downlink and uplink BWP operation;
- SIB-defined common physical configuration;
- PDCCH/PDSCH/PUSCH/PUCCH related configuration;
- CSI measurement and reporting configuration;
- SRS configuration;
- beam management and spatial-relation related configuration;
- measurement objects, reporting quantities, and measurement gaps;
- carrier aggregation, dual connectivity, and cell activation/deactivation-related configuration.

PHY supplies measurements and radio indications used by RRC, such as measurements supporting mobility, beam management, radio link monitoring, and reporting. RRC evaluates the configured measurement/reporting conditions and sends measurement reports to the network when their event or periodic conditions are satisfied.

## 6. Transport of RRC messages through lower layers

RRC messages are mapped to logical channels, then transported by lower layers:

| RRC usage | Logical channel | Typical purpose |
|---|---|---|
| Broadcast RRC information | BCCH | MIB and system information |
| Paging | PCCH | Paging information |
| Initial common RRC signalling | CCCH | Setup/resume/re-establishment related common signalling |
| Dedicated RRC and NAS transport | DCCH | Dedicated signalling over SRBs |

MAC maps these logical channels to the appropriate transport channels, and PHY transmits/receives them over the radio interface.

## 7. Overall role

RRC is therefore the control coordinator of the radio stack:

- it receives system information, paging, common and dedicated signalling from the lower layers;
- it exchanges NAS information with NAS;
- it uses SRBs through PDCP/RLC/MAC/PHY to send and receive dedicated RRC signalling;
- it configures PDCP, RLC, MAC, and PHY entities for SRBs and DRBs;
- it controls radio-resource procedures such as connection establishment, reconfiguration, mobility, measurement reporting, security activation, suspend/resume, and release.

In short, NAS decides or requests higher-level service and mobility actions, RRC translates the required radio-access actions into configurations and procedures, and PDCP/RLC/MAC/PHY implement the configured signalling and radio transport.
